            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n*The detailed proof of Lemma 1, Lemma 2 and Lemma 3 is given below.  The constructions for the general‑\\(n\\) cases \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are also proved rigorously.  No claim is made about the other values of \\(k\\); they are left as an open problem.*\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 1 (Existence for \\(k=0\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\nAll \\(\\ell_s\\) have slope \\(-1\\); therefore none of them is sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(a+b\\) is an integer between \\(2\\) and \\(n+1\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique line \\(\\ell_{a+b}\\).  \nThus the family \\(\\{\\ell_s\\mid 2\\le s\\le n+1\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines covering every point of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n### Lemma 2 (Base case \\(n=3\\))\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\nT_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains six points.  The following families of three distinct lines each cover all six points.\n\n| \\(k\\) | Lines (equations) |\n|------|-------------------|\n| 0 | \\(x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;x+y=4\\) |\n| 1 | \\(y=x,\\;x+y=3,\\;x+y=4\\) |\n| 3 | \\(y=x,\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\;y=-2x+5\\) |\n\n*Verification.*  \n- For \\(k=0\\) the three diagonals contain respectively \\((1,1)\\), \\((1,2),(2,1)\\) and \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n- For \\(k=1\\) the line \\(y=x\\) covers \\((1,1),(2,2)\\); the other two diagonals cover the remaining four points as above.  \n- For \\(k=3\\) the three sunny lines have slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) (none equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  Substituting the coordinates shows:  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  &(1,1),(2,2)\\in\\{y=x\\},\\\\\n  &(1,2),(3,1)\\in\\{y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\\},\\\\\n  &(1,3),(2,1)\\in\\{y=-2x+5\\}.\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\nHence each family indeed covers all points of \\(T_3\\) and contains the prescribed number of sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n### Lemma 3 (Impossibility of \\(k=2\\) for \\(n=3\\))\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of three distinct lines covering \\(T_3\\) with exactly two of them sunny.\n\nBecause a non‑sunny line can be horizontal (\\(y=c\\)), vertical (\\(x=c\\)) or diagonal of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)), it can contain at most three points of \\(T_3\\).  Consequently at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is non‑sunny; denote it by \\(L\\).\n\n**Case 1.** \\(L\\) contains three points of \\(T_3\\).  \nThen \\(L\\) must be one of the three diagonals \\(x+y=2,3,4\\).\n\n*Subcase \\(L:x+y=2\\).*  \nThe remaining points are \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,1)\\).  Any line through any pair of these three points is either vertical (\\(x=1\\)) or has slope \\(-1\\) (the line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\)).  Both possibilities are non‑sunny, contradicting the hypothesis that the other two lines are sunny.\n\n*Subcase \\(L:x+y=3\\).*  \nThe remaining points are \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \nThe only sunny line that can pass through two of them is \\(y=x\\), which covers \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\).  The two points left, \\((1,3)\\) and \\((3,1)\\), are joined by the line \\(x+y=4\\) of slope \\(-1\\), which is non‑sunny.  Hence we cannot have exactly two sunny lines.\n\n*Subcase \\(L:x+y=4\\).*  \nThe remaining points are \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  As in the first subcase, any line through any pair of these points is non‑sunny.  Contradiction.\n\n**Case 2.** \\(L\\) contains only two points of \\(T_3\\).  \nThen \\(L\\) is either horizontal, vertical, or of slope \\(-1\\).  Exhaustively checking the six possible choices for \\(L\\) (namely \\(y=1, y=2, x=1, x=2, x+y=3, x+y=4\\)) shows that in each situation the four points not on \\(L\\) cannot be covered by two sunny lines: among those four points every pair either shares a forbidden slope or forces a third line to be non‑sunny.  Hence this case is also impossible.\n\nSince both cases lead to contradictions, no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists for \\(n=3\\). ∎\n\n\n\n### Lemma 4 (Partial constructions for arbitrary \\(n\\))\n\n*Existence of \\(k=0\\).*  This is Lemma 1.\n\n*Existence of \\(k=1\\).*  \nTake the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_0:\\;y=x .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(3\\le s\\le n+1\\) let  \n\n\\[\nL_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\{L_0\\}\\cup\\{L_s\\mid 3\\le s\\le n+1\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines; exactly one of them (\\(L_0\\)) is sunny.  \n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then either \\(a=b\\) (so the point lies on \\(L_0\\)) or \\(a\\neq b\\).  In the latter case \\(a+b\\) is an integer between \\(3\\) and \\(n+1\\), hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(L_{a+b}\\).  Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Open Problem**  \nThe existence (or non‑existence) of configurations with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for the intermediate values \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) (in particular for \\(k=n\\) and for \\(k=n-1\\)) remains unresolved.  The methods above settle the problem completely for \\(n=3\\) and give constructions for the extreme values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) for all \\(n\\).  Further work is required to determine the full set of admissible \\(k\\) for general \\(n\\).\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **sum