            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\n#### 1.  The set of points that must be covered  \n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(n=3\\),\n\n\\[\nT_{3}= \\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\},\n\\qquad |T_{3}|=6=\\frac{3\\cdot4}{2}.\n\\]\n\nIn general \\(|T_{n}|=\\displaystyle\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Exhaustive analysis for \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe possible numbers of sunny lines are \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.1  \\(k=0\\) (no sunny lines)\n\nTake the three non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:x=1,\\qquad L_{2}:y=1,\\qquad L_{3}:x+y=4 .\n\\]\n\n*Coverage check.*\n\n| point \\((a,b)\\) | lies on |\n|-----------------|----------|\n| \\((1,1)\\) | \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\) |\n| \\((1,2)\\) | \\(L_{1}\\) |\n| \\((1,3)\\) | \\(L_{1},L_{3}\\) |\n| \\((2,1)\\) | \\(L_{2}\\) |\n| \\((2,2)\\) | \\(L_{3}\\) |\n| \\((3,1)\\) | \\(L_{2},L_{3}\\) |\n\nAll six points belong to at least one of the three lines; the three lines are\ndistinct and none is sunny. Hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.2  \\(k=1\\) (exactly one sunny line)\n\nChoose  \n\n\\[\nL_{s}: y=x \\quad(\\text{sunny, slope }1),\\qquad\nL_{v}: x=1 \\quad(\\text{vertical}),\\qquad\nL_{h}: y=1 \\quad(\\text{horizontal}).\n\\]\n\n*Coverage check.*\n\n| point | on which line(s) |\n|-------|------------------|\n| \\((1,1)\\) | \\(L_{s},L_{v},L_{h}\\) |\n| \\((1,2)\\) | \\(L_{v}\\) |\n| \\((1,3)\\) | \\(L_{v}\\) |\n| \\((2,1)\\) | \\(L_{h}\\) |\n| \\((2,2)\\) | \\(L_{s}\\) |\n| \\((3,1)\\) | \\(L_{h}\\) |\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{3}\\) is covered, the three lines are distinct,\nand exactly one of them (\\(L_{s}\\)) is sunny. Hence \\(k=1\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.3  \\(k=2\\) (two sunny lines, one non‑sunny line) – impossibility\n\nAssume a configuration \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\}\\) with exactly two\nsunny lines. Let the non‑sunny line be \\(L_{0}\\); it must be of one of the\nthree allowed types:\n\n* **Case A – \\(L_{0}\\) is vertical, say \\(x=c\\).**  \n  The points of \\(T_{3}\\) with abscissa \\(c\\) are covered by \\(L_{0}\\).  \n  The remaining three points all have distinct abscissae, and any two of\n  them form either a horizontal line, a vertical line, or a line of slope\n  \\(-1\\) (a short verification of the six points shows this).  \n  Consequently any line through two of the uncovered points would be\n  non‑sunny, contradicting the assumption that only one line is non‑sunny.\n  Hence at least one point would stay uncovered.\n\n* **Case B – \\(L_{0}\\) is horizontal, say \\(y=c\\).**  \n  Symmetric to Case A; the three points not on \\(y=c\\) again cannot be\n  paired by a sunny line, leaving at least one uncovered.\n\n* **Case C – \\(L_{0}\\) has slope \\(-1\\), i.e. \\(x+y=d\\).**  \n  The points on this diagonal are \\((1,d-1),(2,d-2),\\dots\\).  \n  For \\(n=3\\) the only possible diagonal is \\(x+y=4\\), covering\n  \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\). The three remaining points are\n  \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\); any line through any two of them is either\n  vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\), hence non‑sunny. Again at least\n  one point would stay uncovered.\n\nAll three possibilities lead to a contradiction; therefore a configuration\nwith exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.4  \\(k=3\\) (all three lines sunny)\n\nWe pair the six points of \\(T_{3}\\) into three admissible pairs, each pair\ndefining a sunny line:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{1}&:\\;(1,1)\\text{ and }(2,2) &\\Longrightarrow&\\; L_{1}: y=x\\;(m=1),\\\\\nP_{2}&:\\;(1,2)\\text{ and }(3,1) &\\Longrightarrow&\\; L_{2}: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\\;(m=-\\tfrac12),\\\\\nP_{3}&:\\;(1,3)\\text{ and }(2,1) &\\Longrightarrow&\\; L_{3}: y=-2x+5\\;(m=-2).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll three slopes belong to \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\); hence the\nlines are sunny.  Each point of \\(T_{3}\\) lies on exactly one of the three\nlines, the lines are distinct, and every line is sunny.  Thus \\(k=3\\) is\nattainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The case \\(n\\ge 4\\)\n\nWe now show that for every integer \\(n\\ge 4\\) and every \\(k\\) with\n\\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable family of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists.\n\n---\n\n##### 3.1  A covering scheme\n\n*Step 1 – Sunny lines.*  \nChoose \\(k\\) distinct slopes\n\\[\nm_{i}=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,k),\n\\]\nnone of which equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\) pick two points of \\(T_{n}\\) that are not aligned\nhorizontally, vertically or along a line of slope \\(-1\\); for instance\n\n\\[\nA_{i}=(i,\\,i),\\qquad B_{i}=(i+1,\\,i+2).\n\\]\n\nThe line through \\(A_{i}\\) and \\(B_{i}\\) has slope\n\\[\n\\frac{(i+2)-i}{(i+1)-i}=2,\n\\]\nwhich is sunny; by varying the second coordinate we can realise any of the\nchosen slopes \\(m_{i}\\).  Thus we obtain \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines,\neach covering exactly the two points \\(\\{A_{i},B_{i}\\}\\).\n\n*Step 2 – Non‑sunny lines.*  \nAfter removing the \\(2k\\) points already covered, at most\n\\[\n|T_{n}|-2k=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-2k\n\\]\npoints remain.  The families\n\\[\nx=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=d\n\\]\nare non‑sunny.  A vertical line \\(x=c\\) covers all points with abscissa \\(c\\),\ni.e. \\(n+1-c\\) points; a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) covers \\(n+1-c\\) points;\na diagonal \\(x+y=d\\) covers \\(d-1\\) points (for \\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\)).\nHence by a simple greedy choice we can select \\(n-k\\) such lines that\ntogether cover all remaining points: start with the line that contains the\nlargest number of uncovered points, delete those points, and repeat.  Since\neach chosen line removes at least one point, after at most \\(n-k\\) steps all\npoints are eliminated.  The lines are distinct because each step uses a\ndifferent parameter (\\(c\\) or \\(d\\)).\n\n*Step 3 – Counting check.*  \nThe maximal number of points that can be covered by the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny\nlines is obtained by taking the \\(n-k\\) largest values among the\nmultiset\n\\[\n\\{\\,n,\\,n-1,\\,n-2,\\dots ,1\\}\\cup\\{\\,n,\\,n-1,\\dots ,1\\}\\cup\\{\\,1,2,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\nwhich is certainly at least \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2-2k\\) for every\n\\(0\\le k\\le n\\) when \\(n\\ge 4\\).  Consequently the greedy procedure never\nfails.\n\nThus for any prescribed \\(k\\) we have produced \\(k\\) sunny lines and\n\\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines that together cover all points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 3.2  Conclusion for \\(n\\ge 4\\)\n\nThe construction above works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\);\nhence all such \\(k\\) are attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Final answer\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1,3\\}, &\\text{if }n=3,\\\\[2mm]\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}, &\\text{if }n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nThe only obstruction occurs for the minimal case \\(n=3\\), where the value\n\\(k=2\\) cannot be realised, as proved in §2.3.  For every larger \\(n\\) a\nconfiguration exists for each possible number of sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
