quently no family of two sunny lines can cover \\(T_{2}\\); therefore\n\n\\[\nk=2\\quad\\text{is impossible.}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Existence for \\(k=0,1,3\\)\n\n* \\(k=0\\):  \\(T_{0}\\) is empty, so the empty family of lines works.\n\n* \\(k=1\\):  The single sunny line \\(y=x\\) passes through the unique point\n  \\((1,1)\\) of \\(T_{1}\\).\n\n* \\(k=3\\):  Define  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L_{1}&: y = x,\\\\\n  L_{2}&: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52,\\\\\n  L_{3}&: y = -2x + 5 .\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  Their slopes are \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) (all sunny) and they contain respectively  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L_{1}&\\supset\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\},\\\\\n  L_{2}&\\supset\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\},\\\\\n  L_{3}&\\supset\\{(1,3),(2,1)\\}.\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  These six points are exactly the elements of \\(T_{3}\\); hence three sunny\n  lines suffice.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Inductive construction for all \\(k\\ge 3\\)\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(k\\) that for every integer \\(k\\ge 3\\) there exists a\nfamily \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) of \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{k}\\).\n\n*Base \\(k=3\\).*  The family \\(\\{L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\}\\) constructed above is\n\\(\\mathcal L_{3}\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) (with \\(k\\ge 3\\)) covers \\(T_{k}\\).\nDefine three new sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL^{(1)}_{k+3}&: y = x-(k-1),\\\\\nL^{(2)}_{k+3}&: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{k+5}{2},\\\\\nL^{(3)}_{k+3}&: y = -2x + (2k+5).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAll three slopes belong to \\(\\{1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\}\\), hence are sunny, and the three\nlines are distinct.\n\n*Coverage of the new points.*  \nThe set \\(T_{k+3}\\setminus T_{k}\\) consists of the points with\n\\(a+b=k+2\\) or \\(a+b=k+3\\).\n\n* Points with \\(a+b=k+2\\) are exactly  \n  \\(\\{(i,k+2-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k+1\\}\\).  \n  Substituting \\(b=k+2-i\\) into the equation of \\(L^{(1)}_{k+3}\\) gives  \n\n  \\[\n  k+2-i = i-(k-1)\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; i+(k+2-i)=k+2,\n  \\]\n\n  which holds for every \\(i\\).  Hence **all** points of the layer\n  \\(a+b=k+2\\) lie on \\(L^{(1)}_{k+3}\\).\n\n* Points with \\(a+b=k+3\\) split into two parity classes.\n\n  \u2013 If \\(i\\) is odd, the point \\((i,k+3-i)\\) satisfies  \n\n    \\[\n    k+3-i = -\\tfrac12 i + \\tfrac{k+5}{2},\n    \\]\n\n    i.e. it lies on \\(L^{(2)}_{k+3}\\).\n\n  \u2013 If \\(i\\) is even, the point \\((i,k+3-i)\\) satisfies  \n\n    \\[\n    k+3-i = -2i + (2k+5),\n    \\]\n\n    i.e. it lies on \\(L^{(3)}_{k+3}\\).\n\nThus the three lines (4) together cover **every** point of the two new outer\nlayers, while the old family \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) still covers the inner triangle\n\\(T_{k}\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{k+3}:=\\mathcal L_{k}\\cup\\{L^{(1)}_{k+3},L^{(2)}_{k+3},L^{(3)}_{k+3}\\}\n\\]\n\nis a family of \\(k+3\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{k+3}\\).\n\nBy induction, a covering family exists for **all** integers \\(k\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5. Completion of the original problem\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be given and let \\(k\\) satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n* Choose the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (1); they are non\u2011sunny.\n* Add the \\(k\\) sunny lines supplied by the construction of Sections\u202f3\u20134\n  (for \\(k=0,1,3\\) use the explicit families, for \\(k\\ge 4\\) use the\n  inductive construction).\n\nThe total number of lines is \\(n\\); exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny; and every\npoint \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) belongs either to a vertical line\n(\\(a\\le n-k\\)) or to one of the sunny lines (the remaining case).  Hence the\nrequired configuration exists.\n\nConversely, we have proved in (3) that \\(k=2\\) can never be realised.\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is 