            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Reduction\n\nFix \\(n\\ge 3\\) and a non‑negative integer \\(k\\le n\\).\nChoose \\(n-k\\) distinct vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) with abscissa \\(a\\le n-k\\) lies on one of the lines\n(1).  The points that are not covered are precisely  \n\n\\[\nU_{k}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(a' = a-(n-k)\\) and \\(b'=b\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nU_{k}= \\{(a',b')\\mid a',b'\\ge 1,\\;a'+b'\\le k+1\\}=T_{k}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus the original problem is equivalent to the following sub‑problem.\n\n> **Sub‑problem.**  \n> For which integers \\(k\\ge 0\\) does there exist a family of exactly \\(k\\)\n> sunny lines covering all points of \\(T_{k}\\)?\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Impossibility for \\(k=2\\)\n\n\\(T_{2}=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).\n\n*Any sunny line through \\((1,1)\\) has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\);*\nthe only other points of \\(T_{2}\\) are \\((1,2)\\) (vertical) and \\((2,1)\\)\n(horizontal).  Hence a sunny line containing \\((1,1)\\) cannot contain any\nother point of \\(T_{2}\\).\n\nThe remaining two points \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) lie on the line\n\\(x+y=3\\), whose slope is \\(-1\\) and therefore is **not** sunny.\n\nConsequently no family of two sunny lines can cover \\(T_{2}\\); therefore\n\n\\[\nk=2\\quad\\text{is impossible.}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Existence for \\(k=0,1,3\\)\n\n* \\(k=0\\):  \\(T_{0}\\) is empty, so the empty family of lines works.\n\n* \\(k=1\\):  The single sunny line \\(y=x\\) passes through the unique point\n  \\((1,1)\\) of \\(T_{1}\\).\n\n* \\(k=3\\):  Define  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L_{1}&: y = x,\\\\\n  L_{2}&: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52,\\\\\n  L_{3}&: y = -2x + 5 .\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  Their slopes are \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) (all sunny) and they contain respectively  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L_{1}&\\supset\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\},\\\\\n  L_{2}&\\supset\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\},\\\\\n  L_{3}&\\supset\\{(1,3),(2,1)\\}.\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  These six points are exactly the elements of \\(T_{3}\\); hence three sunny\n  lines suffice.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Inductive construction for all \\(k\\ge 3\\)\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(k\\) that for every integer \\(k\\ge 3\\) there exists a\nfamily \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) of \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{k}\\).\n\n*Base \\(k=3\\).*  The family \\(\\{L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\}\\) constructed above is\n\\(\\mathcal L_{3}\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) (with \\(k\\ge 3\\)) covers \\(T_{k}\\).\nDefine three new sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL^{(1)}_{k+3}&: y = x-(k-1),\\\\\nL^{(2)}_{k+3}&: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{k+5}{2},\\\\\nL^{(3)}_{k+3}&: y = -2x + (2k+5).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAll three slopes belong to \\(\\{1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\}\\), hence are sunny, and the three\nlines are distinct.\n\n*Coverage of the new points.*  \nThe set \\(T_{k+3}\\setminus T_{k}\\) consists of the points with\n\\(a+b=k+2\\) or \\(a+b=k+3\\).\n\n* Points with \\(a+b=k+2\\) are exactly  \n  \\(\\{(i,k+2-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k+1\\}\\).  \n  Substituting \\(b=k+2-i\\) into the equation of \\(L^{(1)}_{k+3}\\) gives  \n\n  \\[\n  k+2-i = i-(k-1)\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; i+(k+2-i)=k+2,\n  \\]\n\n  which holds for every \\(i\\).  Hence **all** points of the layer\n  \\(a+b=k+2\\) lie on \\(L^{(1)}_{k+3}\\).\n\n* Points with \\(a+b=k+3\\) split into two parity classes.\n\n  – If \\(i\\) is odd, the point \\((i,k+3-i)\\) satisfies  \n\n    \\[\n    k+3-i = -\\tfrac12 i + \\tfrac{k+5}{2},\n    \\]\n\n    i.e. it lies on \\(L^{(2)}_{k+3}\\).\n\n  – If \\(i\\) is even, the point \\((i,k+3-i)\\) satisfies  \n\n    \\[\n    k+3-i = -2i + (2k+5),\n    \\]\n\n    i.e. it lies on \\(L^{(3)}_{k+3}\\).\n\nThus the three lines (4) together cover **every** point of the two new outer\nlayers, while the old family \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) still covers the inner triangle\n\\(T_{k}\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{k+3}:=\\mathcal L_{k}\\cup\\{L^{(1)}_{k+3},L^{(2)}_{k+3},L^{(3)}_{k+3}\\}\n\\]\n\nis a family of \\(k+3\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{k+3}\\).\n\nBy induction, a covering family exists for **all** integers \\(k\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5. Completion of the original problem\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be given and let \\(k\\) satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n* Choose the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (1); they are non‑sunny.\n* Add the \\(k\\) sunny lines supplied by the construction of Sections 3–4\n  (for \\(k=0,1,3\\) use the explicit families, for \\(k\\ge 4\\) use the\n  inductive construction).\n\nThe total number of lines is \\(n\\); exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny; and every\npoint \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) belongs either to a vertical line\n(\\(a\\le n-k\\)) or to one of the sunny lines (the remaining case).  Hence the\nrequired configuration exists.\n\nConversely, we have proved in (3) that \\(k=2\\) can never be realised.\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely the set described\nin (5).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Final answer:** For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nand no other non‑negative integer \\(k\\) admits a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines satisfying the two stated conditions. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
