ealised is \\(k=2\\); all other integers \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) are attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\n#### 1. Notation and reduction\n\nFor an integer \\(m\\ge 0\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_m=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le m+1\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope belongs to \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).\n\nFix \\(n\\ge 3\\) and a non‑negative integer \\(k\\le n\\).  \nChoose the \\(n-k\\) distinct vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\) lies on one of the lines (2).  \nThe points that are not covered are\n\n\\[\nU_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(a'=a-(n-k)\\) and \\(b'=b\\); then \\(a',b'\\ge 1\\) and  \n\n\\[\na'+b' = a+b-(n-k)\\le (n+1)-(n-k)=k+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nU_k=\\{(a',b')\\mid a',b'\\ge 1,\\;a'+b'\\le k+1\\}=T_k .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the original problem is equivalent to the following sub‑problem.\n\n> **Sub‑problem.**  \n> For which integers \\(k\\ge 0\\) does there exist a family of exactly \\(k\\) sunny\n> lines covering all points of \\(T_k\\)?\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Impossibility for \\(k=2\\)\n\n\\(T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).\n\nA sunny line has slope \\(\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nAny sunny line through \\((1,1)\\) therefore has equation  \n\n\\[\ny-1=m(x-1),\\qquad m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nPlugging \\((1,2)\\) gives \\(2-1=m(1-1)=0\\), impossible; similarly \\((2,1)\\) does not satisfy the equation.  \nHence a sunny line containing \\((1,1)\\) cannot contain any other point of \\(T_2\\).\n\nThe remaining two points \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) lie on the line  \n\n\\[\nx+y=3\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y=-x+3,\n\\]\n\nwhose slope is \\(-1\\) and therefore is **not** sunny.  \nThus no family of two sunny lines can cover \\(T_2\\); conseq