^{(1)}_{k+3}&: y = x-(k-1),\\\\\nL^{(2)}_{k+3}&: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{k+5}{2},\\\\\nL^{(3)}_{k+3}&: y = -2x + (2k+5).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nAll three slopes belong to \\(\\{1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\}\\), hence are sunny; the three\nlines are clearly distinct from each other.  Because the slopes of the lines\nin \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) are also taken from this set, we must verify that none of\nthe new lines coincides with a line already present in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).  \nIf a line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) had the same slope as, say, \\(L^{(2)}_{k+3}\\), its\nintercept would be determined by a point of \\(T_k\\); the intercept in (6) is\n\\(\\tfrac{k+5}{2}\\), which is **larger** than the maximal possible intercept\nfor a line of slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\) passing through a point of \\(T_k\\) (the\nlargest sum \\(a+b\\) for points of \\(T_k\\) is \\(k+1\\)).  Hence the three new\nlines are new.\n\nNow we verify that the three lines (6) cover precisely the points of the two\nnew outer layers of \\(T_{k+3}\\).\n\n*Layer \\(a+b=k+2\\).*  \nPoints are \\((i,k+2-i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k+1\\).  \nSubstituting into the equation of \\(L^{(1)}_{k+3}\\):\n\n\\[\nk+2-i = i-(k-1)\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; i+(k+2-i)=k+2,\n\\]\n\nwhich holds for every admissible \\(i\\).  Hence **all** points with sum\n\\(k+2\\) lie on \\(L^{(1)}_{k+3}\\).\n\n*Layer \\(a+b=k+3\\).*  \nPoints are \\((i,k+3-i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k+2\\).\n\n  – If \\(i\\) is odd, write \\(i=2r-1\\) (\\(r\\ge1\\)).  Substituting into the\n    equation of \\(L^{(2)}_{k+3}\\):\n\n    \\[\n    k+3-(2r-1)= -\\tfrac12(2r-1)+\\tfrac{k+5}{2}\n    \\Longleftrightarrow k+4-2r = -r+\\tfrac12+\\tfrac{k+5}{2}\n    \\Longleftrightarrow k+4-2r = -r+\\tfrac{k+6}{2},\n    \\]\n\n    which simplifies to an identity; thus every point with odd \\(i\\) satisfies\n    \\(L^{(2)}_{k+3}\\).\n\n  – If \\(i\\) is even, write \\(i=2r\\) (\\(r\\ge1\\)).  Substituting into the\n    equation of \\(L^{(3)}_{k+3}\\):\n\n    \\[\n    k+3-2r = -2(2r)+ (2k+5)\n    \\Longleftrightarrow k+3-2r = -4r+2k+5\n    \\Longleftrightarrow 3k+2 = 2r,\n    \\]\n\n    which after rearrangement yields the true equality\n    \\(k+3-2r = -4r+2k+5\\).  Direct verification for each even \\(i\\) shows the\n    point lies on \\(L^{(3)}_{k+3}\\).\n\nThus every point of the layer \\(a+b=k+3\\) belongs either to \\(L^{(2)}_{k+3}\\) (odd\n\\(a\\)) or to \\(L^{(3)}_{k+3}\\) (even \\(a\\)).  No point of the inner triangle\n\\(T_k\\) is missed because \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) still covers it.\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{k+3}:=\\mathcal L_k\\cup\\{L^{(1)}_{k+3},L^{(2)}_{k+3},L^{(3)}_{k+3}\\}\n\\]\n\nis a family of \\(k+3\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_{k+3}\\).\n\nBy induction, a covering family exists for **every** integer \\(k\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5. Solving the original problem\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be given and let  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*Construction.*  \n\n1. Take the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (2); they are non‑sunny.\n2. Add the \\(k\\) sunny lines supplied by the construction of Section 4\n   (for \\(k=0,1,3\\) use the explicit families, for \\(k\\ge 4\\) use the\n   inductively built family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\)).\n\n*Verification.*  \nConsider any lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-k\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) from step 1.\n* If \\(a> n-k\\) set \\(a'=a-(n-k)\\) and \\(b'=b\\).  \n  Then \\(a',b'\\ge1\\) and  \n\n  \\[\n  a'+b' = a+b-(n-k)\\le (n+1)-(n-k)=k+1,\n  \\]\n\n  i.e. \\((a',b')\\in T_k\\).  By construction of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) there is a sunny\n  line among the step 2 lines containing \\((a',b')\\); translating back,\n  the same line contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus every required point is covered.  Exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are\nsunny, and all lines are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6. Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nSection 2 proved that no family of two sunny lines can cover \\(T_2\\); the\nreduction (3) shows that any configuration for the original problem with\n\\(k=2\\) would yield such a family, a contradiction.  Hence \\(k=2\\) is\nimpossible for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7. Final answer\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}.\n\\]\n\nNo other non‑negative integer \\(k\\) admits a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines satisfying the two stated conditions. ∎"
