   L_3 &: \\{(1,3),(2,1)\\},\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   which are exactly the six points of \\(T_3\\).\n\n5. **Inductive step \\(k\\mapsto k+3\\) (for all \\(k\\ge 3\\)).**  \n   Assuming a family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_k\\), add the three new sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L^{(1)}_{k+3}&: y = x-(k-1),\\\\\n   L^{(2)}_{k+3}&: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{k+5}{2},\\\\\n   L^{(3)}_{k+3}&: y = -2x + (2k+5),\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{\u2605}\n   \\]\n\n   and verify **explicitly** that:\n\n   * every point of the new outer layer \\(a+b=k+2\\) lies on \\(L^{(1)}_{k+3}\\);\n   * every point of the outer layer \\(a+b=k+3\\) with odd \\(a\\) lies on \\(L^{(2)}_{k+3}\\);\n   * every point of the outer layer \\(a+b=k+3\\) with even \\(a\\) lies on \\(L^{(3)}_{k+3}\\).\n\n   The three lines in (\u2605) have slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) (all sunny) and are distinct from each other and from the lines already present in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).  \n   Hence \\(\\mathcal L_{k+3}:=\\mathcal L_k\\cup\\{L^{(1)}_{k+3},L^{(2)}_{k+3},L^{(3)}_{k+3}\\}\\) is a family of \\(k+3\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{k+3}\\).\n\n   Starting from the explicit construction for \\(k=3\\) and iterating the step, we obtain a covering family for **every** integer \\(k\\ge 3\\).\n\n6. **Lifting to the original parameter \\(n\\).**  \n   For any admissible \\(k\\) (i.e. \\(k=0,1\\) or \\(k\\ge 3\\) with \\(k\\le n\\)):\n\n   * take the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) (non\u2011sunny);\n   * add the \\(k\\) sunny lines supplied by the construction of step\u202f4 (or the base cases).\n\n   Every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) either satisfies \\(a\\le n-k\\) (hence lies on a vertical line) or, after the translation \\((a',b')=(a-(n-k),b)\\), belongs to \\(T_k\\) and is therefore covered by one of the sunny lines.  \n   Exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny, and all required points are covered.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The only value of \\(k\\) that cannot be realised is \\(k=2\\); all other integers \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) are attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\n#### 1. Notation and reduction\n\nFor an integer \\(m\\ge 0\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_m=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le m+1\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope belongs to \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).\n\nFix \\(n\\ge 3\\) and a non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\le n\\).  \nChoose the \\(n-k\\) distinct vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\) lies on one of the lines (2).  \nThe points that are not covered are\n\n\\[\nU_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(a'=a-(n-k)\\) and \\(b'=b\\); then \\(a',b'\\ge 1\\) and  \n\n\\[\na'+b' = a+b-(n-k)\\le (n+1)-(n-k)=k+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nU_k=\\{(a',b')\\mid a',b'\\ge 1,\\;a'+b'\\le k+1\\}=T_k .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the original problem is equivalent to the following sub\u2011problem.\n\n> **Sub\u2011problem.**  \n> For which integers \\(k\\ge 0\\) does there exist a family of exactly \\(k\\) sunny\n> lines covering all points of \\(T_k\\)?\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Impossibility for \\(k=2\\)\n\n\\(T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).\n\nA sunny line has slope \\(\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nAny sunny line through \\((1,1)\\) therefore has equation  \n\n\\[\ny-1=m(x-1),\\qquad m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nPlugging \\((1,2)\\) gives \\(2-1=m(1-1)=0\\), impossible; similarly \\((2,1)\\) does not satisfy the equation.  \nHence a sunny line containing \\((1,1)\\) cannot contain any other point of \\(T_2\\).\n\nThe remaining two points \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) lie on the line  \n\n\\[\nx+y=3\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y=-x+3,\n\\]\n\nwhose slope is \\(-1\\) and therefore is **not** sunny.  \nThus no family of two sunny lines can cover \\(T_2\\); consequently\n\n\\[\nk=2\\text{ is impossible.}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Existence for the base cases \\(k=0,1,3\\)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \\(T_0=\\varnothing\\); the empty family of lines works.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  The line  \n\n  \\[\n  L_1:\\; y=x\n  \\]\n\n  has slope \\(1\\) (sunny) and contains the unique point \\((1,1)\\) of \\(T_1\\).\n\n* **\\(k=3\\).**  Consider  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L_1 &: y = x,\\\\\n  L_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52,\\\\\n  L_3 &: y = -2x + 5 .\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\tag{5}\n  \\]\n\n  Their slopes are \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\), all sunny, and the three lines are distinct.\n  Verify point\u2011by\u2011point that they contain exactly the six points of \\(T_3\\).\n\n  *For \\(L_1\\):*  \n  Substituting \\((1,1)\\) gives \\(1=1\\); substituting \\((2,2)\\) gives \\(2=2\\).  \n  No other point of \\(T_3\\) satisfies \\(y=x\\) because the remaining points have\n  unequal coordinates.\n\n  *For \\(L_2\\):*  \n  \\((1,2)\\): \\(2 = -\\tfrac12\\cdot1 + \\tfrac52 = 2\\) \u2713  \n  \\((3,1)\\): \\(1 = -\\tfrac12\\cdot3 + \\tfrac52 = 1\\) \u2713  \n  The other four points of \\(T_3\\) do not satisfy the equation (direct substitution\n  shows a mismatch).\n\n  *For \\(L_3\\):*  \n  \\((1,3)\\): \\(3 = -2\\cdot1 +5 = 3\\) \u2713  \n  \\((2,1)\\): \\(1 = -2\\cdot2 +5 = 1\\) \u2713  \n  Again the remaining points fail the equation.\n\n  Hence the three sunny lines (5) together cover all points of \\(T_3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Inductive construction for all \\(k\\ge 3\\)\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(k\\) that for every integer \\(k\\ge 3\\) there exists a\nfamily \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_k\\).\n\n*Base.*  \\(\\mathcal L_3=\\{L_1,L_2,L_3\\}\\) from the previous paragraph.\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) (wit