            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be fixed.  \nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Reduction to a triangular lattice\n\nChoose any \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines and make them vertical:\n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are pairwise distinct and none of them is sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\) from (1).\n\nA point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) satisfies\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\); putting \\(a'=a-(n-k)\\) and \\(b'=b\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\na',b'>0,\\qquad a'+b' = a+b-(n-k)\\le (n+1)-(n-k)=k+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the set of points that are **not** covered by the vertical lines (1) is\n\n\\[\nT_k:=\\{(a',b')\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a'+b'\\le k+1\\}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the original problem is equivalent to the following:\n\n> **Sub‑problem.** For a given integer \\(k\\ge 0\\) find \\(k\\) distinct sunny\n> lines that together contain every point of the finite set \\(T_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The case \\(k=2\\) is impossible\n\n\\(T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nAssume two sunny lines \\(L_1,L_2\\) cover \\(T_2\\).\n\n*If a sunny line passes through \\((1,1)\\), its slope cannot be \\(0\\) (horizontal)\nnor \\(\\infty\\) (vertical); therefore it cannot contain \\((1,2)\\) nor\n\\((2,1)\\).  Hence the second line must contain both \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).\n\nThe unique line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) is \\(x+y=3\\), whose slope is\n\\(-1\\); this line is **not** sunny.  Contradiction.\n\nThus no arrangement with exactly two sunny lines can satisfy the covering\ncondition, i.e. **\\(k=2\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines (1) with \\(k=0\\).  \nEvery required point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a\\le n\\), hence lies on the line\n\\(x=a\\).  No sunny line is used, so \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction for \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and the single sunny line\n\n\\[\nL:\\; y = -\\frac{1}{\\,n-1\\,}\\,x + \\frac{n}{\\,n-1\\,}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAll points with first coordinate \\(a\\le n-1\\) are covered by the vertical\nlines.  The only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\); substituting \\((n,1)\\) into\n(3) gives equality, so \\((n,1)\\in L\\).  The slope \\(-\\frac{1}{n-1}\\) is\ndifferent from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(L\\) is sunny.  Hence \\(k=1\\) is\nrealizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Construction for any \\(k\\ge 3\\)\n\nKeep the vertical lines (1).  It remains to cover the set \\(T_k\\) defined in\n(2).  For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k\\) define\n\n\\[\nL_t:\\; y = \\frac{t}{\\,k+1-t\\,}\\bigl(x-(n-k)\\bigr)+1 . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Sunnyness.*  The slope of \\(L_t\\) is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{t}{k+1-t}\\).\nBoth numerator and denominator are positive, so the slope is finite,\nnon‑zero and different from \\(-1\\); consequently each \\(L_t\\) is sunny.\nIf \\(t\\neq s\\) then \\(\\frac{t}{k+1-t}\\neq\\frac{s}{k+1-s}\\); thus the lines are\npairwise distinct.\n\n*Coverage of \\(T_k\\).*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\).  Because \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(a\\le k+1\\), we can write\n\\(a=n-k+t\\) for a unique integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k\\).  The condition\n\\(a+b\\le k+1\\) becomes \\(b\\le k+1-a = k+1-(n-k+t)=k+1-t\\).\n\nFix this \\(t\\).  Substituting \\(x=n-k+t\\) into (4) yields\n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{t}{k+1-t}\\,t + 1\n    = \\frac{t^{2}}{k+1-t}+1 . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(1\\le b\\le k+1-t\\), we have\n\n\\[\nb = \\frac{t}{k+1-t}\\bigl((k+1-t)-b\\bigr)+1 .\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives exactly equation (5); therefore the point\n\\((a,b)=(n-k+t,b)\\) satisfies (4) **iff** \\(b\\le k+1-t\\), which is true by\nconstruction.  Hence every point of the column \\(x=n-k+t\\) (i.e. every point\nof \\(T_k\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(n-k+t\\)) lies on the line \\(L_t\\).\n\nBecause the columns \\(x=n-k+1,\\dots ,x=n\\) partition \\(T_k\\), the union of\nthe \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) covers the whole set \\(T_k\\).\n\n*Putting everything together.*  \nThe family consisting of\n\n* the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (1), and  \n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) defined by (4),\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of which are sunny,\nand covers every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) such a configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Final classification\n\n* \\(k=0\\) – possible (step 3).  \n* \\(k=1\\) – possible (step 4).  \n* \\(k=2\\) – impossible (step 2).  \n* \\(k\\ge 3\\) (and \\(k\\le n\\)) – possible (step 5).\n\nHence the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**The problem is completely solved.**\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
