n-2,2),\\;(n-2,1),\\\\\n   &(n-1,2),\\;(n-1,1),\\\\\n   &(n,1),\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the triangle \\(T_3\\).\n\n   *Step 5.2 – Three sunny lines covering \\(T_3\\).*  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: \\text{through }(n-2,3)\\text{ and }(n-1,1)\n          \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=-2x+2n-1,\\\\[2mm]\n   L_2 &: \\text{through }(n-2,2)\\text{ and }(n,1)\n          \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=-\\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac{n}{2}+1,\\\\[2mm]\n   L_3 &: \\text{through }(n-2,1)\\text{ and }(n-1,2)\n          \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y= x-(n-3).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Their slopes are \\(-2,\\,-\\tfrac12,\\;1\\); none equals \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\),\n   so all three are sunny.  Substituting the coordinates from (3) shows\n   that each of the six points lies on one of the lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\).\n\n   *Step 5.3 – Adding extra sunny lines.*  \n   If the required number of sunny lines is \\(k>3\\), introduce\n   \\(k-3\\) further sunny lines that avoid **all** points \\((a,b)\\) with\n   \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  For instance, for each integer \\(t\\ge1\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   S_t:\\; y=2x+(n+10)t .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(2\\) is sunny, and because the intercept exceeds \\(n\\),\n   for every admissible point we have \\(b\\le n\\) while\n   \\(2a+(n+10)t\\ge 2\\cdot1+(n+10) > n+1\\); hence no such point lies on any\n   \\(S_t\\).  Choose \\(k-3\\) distinct values of \\(t\\) to obtain \\(k-3\\) distinct\n   sunny lines.\n\n   *Step 5.4 – Counting.*  \n   The total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-3)\\;(\\text{vertical})\\;+\\;3\\;(\\text{covering }T_3)\\;+\\;(k-3)\n   \\;=\\;n+k-3\\;=\\;n,\n   \\]\n\n   and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  All required points are covered,\n   completing the construction for every \\(k\\ge3\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   * \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable (steps 3 and 4).  \n   * \\(k=2\\) is impossible (step 2).  \n   * Every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable (step 5).\n\n   Hence the set of all admissible \\(k\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\; \\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\; } .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be fixed.  \nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Reduction to a triangular set\n\nChoose \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines and make them the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) satisfies \\(a\\le n\\); therefore\nif \\(a\\le n-k\\) the point lies on the line \\(x=a\\) from (1).\n\nIf \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) write \\(a'=a-(n-k)\\;(1\\le a'\\le k)\\) and keep the same\n\\(b\\).  The inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes  \n\n\\[\na'+b\\le k+1 .\n\\]\n\nHence the points that are **not** covered by the vertical lines (1) are exactly  \n\n\\[\nT_k:=\\{(a',b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a'+b\\le k+1\\}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus the original problem is equivalent to:\n\n> **Sub‑problem.** For a given integer \\(k\\ge0\\) find \\(k\\) distinct sunny\n> lines that together contain every point of the finite set \\(T_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\n\\(T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nAssume two sunny lines \\(L_1,L_2\\) cover these three points.\n\n*If a sunny line passes through \\((1,1)\\), its slope cannot be \\(0\\) (horizontal)\nnor \\(\\infty\\) (vertical); consequently it cannot contain \\((1,2)\\) nor\n\\((2,1)\\).  Hence the second line must contain both \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).\n\nThe unique line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) is \\(x+y=3\\), whose slope is\n\\(-1\\); this line is **not** sunny.  Contradiction.\n\nTherefore two sunny lines can never cover all points of \\(T_2\\); consequently\n\\(k=2\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n\\]\n\nAll are non‑sunny, so exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines are used, and every point\n\\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction for \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1 .\n\\]\n\nAll points with first coordinate \\(\\le n-1\\) are covered.  \nThe only point that may remain uncovered is \\((n,1)\\) (because\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b\\le1\\) when \\(a=n\\)).\n\nConsider the line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\):\n\n\\[\nL:\\; y-1 = \\frac{2-1}{1-n}\\,(x-n)\n   \\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n   y = -\\frac{1}{\\,n-1\\,}x+\\frac{n}{\\,n-1\\,}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIts slope \\(-\\dfrac1{n-1}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(L\\)\nis sunny.  Substituting \\((n,1)\\) into (3) gives \\(1=1\\), so \\((n,1)\\in L\\).\n\nThus we have exactly one sunny line and the required covering.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Construction for any \\(k\\ge 3\\)\n\n##### 5.1  Reduce the uncovered region to a triangle of size 3  \n\nKeep the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-3 . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThese are \\(n-3\\) non‑sunny lines.  After removing them, the points that are\nstill uncovered are precisely the six points\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(n-2,3),\\;(n-2,2),\\;(n-2,1),\\\\\n&(n-1,2),\\;(n-1,1),\\\\\n&(n,1),\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\ni.e. the set \\(T_3\\) (the triangle of size 3).\n\n##### 5.2  Three sunny lines covering \\(T_3\\)\n\nDefine\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: \\text{through }(n-2,3)\\text{ and }(n-1,1)\n      &&\\Longrightarrow\\; y = -2x + 2n - 1,\\\\[2mm]\nL_2 &: \\text{through }(n-2,2)\\text{ and }(n,1)\n      &&\\Longrightarrow\\; y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{n}{2}+1,\\\\[2mm]\nL_3 &: \\text{through }(n-2,1)\\text{ and }(n-1,2)\n      &&\\Longrightarrow\\; y = x-(n-3).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe slopes are \\(-2,\\,-\\tfrac12,\\;1\\); none equals \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\),\nhence each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\nA direct substitution shows:\n\n* \\(L_1\\) contains \\((n-2,3)\\) and \\((n-1,1)\\);\n* \\(L_2\\) contains \\((n-2,2)\\) and \\((n,1)\\);\n* \\(L_3\\) contains \\((n-2,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\).\n\nThus the six points of (5) are all covered by the three sunny lines\n\\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\).\n\n##### 5.3  Adding extra sunny lines when \\(k>3\\)\n\nIf the required number of sunny lines is \\(k>3\\), introduce \\(k-3\\) further\nsunny lines that avoid **every** point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\nFor each integer \\(t\\ge1\\) set  \n\n\\[\nS_t:\\; y = 2x + (n+10)t . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(2\\) is sunny.  Because the intercept \\((n+10)t\\) exceeds \\(n\\),\nfor any admissible point we have  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n < 2a+(n+10)t,\n\\]\n\nso no such point lies on any \\(S_t\\).  Choose \\(k-3\\) distinct values of\n\\(t\\) to obtain \\(k-3\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n##### 5.4  Verification of the count  \n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-3)}_{\\text{vertical}} +\n\\underbrace{3}_{\\text{covering }T_3} +\n\\underbrace{(k-3)}_{\\text{extra}} = n .\n\\]\n\nExactly \\(k\\) of them (the three \\(L_i\\) plus the \\(k-3\\) lines \\(S_t\\))\nare sunny, and every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least\none of the lines.  Hence the construction works for every integer\n\\(k\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Final classification\n\n* \\(k=0\\): vertical‑line construction (Section 3).  \n* \\(k=1\\): one sunny line plus vertical lines (Section 4).  \n* \\(k=2\\): impossible (Section 2).  \n* \\(k\\ge3\\): construction of Section 5.\n\nConsequently the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\; } .\n\\]"
