            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope \\(\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Reduction to a triangular set\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\nPlace the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll of them are non‑sunny.  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(a\\le n\\).  \nIf \\(a\\le n-k\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\) from (1).  \nHence the points that are **not** covered by the lines (1) are precisely\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nU_k&=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a\\ge n-k+1\\}\\\\\n   &=\\{(n-k+i,b)\\mid i\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;n-k+i+b\\le n+1\\}\\\\\n   &=\\{(i,j)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid i+j\\le k+1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus, after the translation \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-(n-k),b)\\), the uncovered\nset is the triangle  \n\n\\[\nT_k:=\\{(i,j)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid i+j\\le k+1\\}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently, to finish the construction it suffices to cover \\(T_k\\) by\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Covering the triangle \\(T_k\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(k\\) that \\(T_k\\) can be covered by exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Base cases.*\n\n* \\(k=0\\): \\(T_0=\\varnothing\\); no line is needed.\n* \\(k=1\\): \\(T_1=\\{(1,1)\\}\\).  The line  \n\n  \\[\n  y=2x-1 \\tag{3}\n  \\]\n\n  has slope \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); it is sunny and contains \\((1,1)\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the statement holds for some \\(k-1\\ge1\\); i.e. there exist\n\\(k-1\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{k-1}\\).\n\nFor \\(T_k\\) we first add the **non‑sunny** diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nD_k:\\;x+y=k+1 . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n\\(D_k\\) contains all points of \\(T_k\\) with \\(x+y=k+1\\); there are exactly\n\\(k\\) such points.  Removing these points leaves the set  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid i+j\\le k\\}=T_{k-1}.\n\\]\n\nBy the induction hypothesis \\(T_{k-1}\\) is covered by \\(k-1\\) sunny\nlines; denote them \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\).\n\nAt this stage we have used \\(k-1\\) sunny lines.  To obtain **exactly**\n\\(k\\) sunny lines we introduce one further sunny line, for instance the\nline (3) used in the base case.  This extra line may intersect points\nalready covered; it does not destroy the covering property.\n\nHence \\(T_k\\) is covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_1,\\dots ,L_{k-1},\\; \\text{(3)} .\n\\]\n\nThe induction is complete, and the claim holds for every \\(k\\ge0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the whole construction\n\nGiven \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we proceed as follows.\n\n1. **Non‑sunny part.**  \n   Take the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (1).  They are distinct and non‑sunny.\n\n2. **Sunny part.**  \n   Apply the construction of Section 2 to the triangle \\(T_k\\); this yields\n   exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that together contain every point of\n   \\(T_k\\).\n\n3. **Verification.**  \n   *If a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(a\\le n-k\\), it lies on the\n   vertical line \\(x=a\\) from step 1.*  \n   *If \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\), then after the translation\n   \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-(n-k),b)\\) the point belongs to \\(T_k\\) and is\n   therefore contained in one of the sunny lines from step 2.*  \n\n   Hence **every** point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.\n\n4. **Counting.**  \n   The total number of lines is \\((n-k)+(k)=n\\); exactly \\(k\\) of them are\n   sunny by construction.\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a configuration satisfying\nthe two conditions of the problem exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Exhaustiveness\n\nThe definition of “exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines’’ forces \\(0\\le k\\le n\\); no\nlarger or negative value can occur.  Since we have exhibited a valid\nconfiguration for **every** integer in this interval, the set of admissible\nvalues of \\(k\\) is precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus the problem is completely solved.**\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
