Let  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A family of non‑sunny vertical lines\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\nDraw the \\(n-k\\) distinct vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nEach of them is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.\nFor any point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\) we have\n\\((a,b)\\in\\) the line \\(x=a\\) of (1).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The uncovered points\n\nThe points of \\(S_n\\) that are **not** on any line of (1) are those with\nfirst coordinate at least \\(n-k+1\\).  Writing \\(a=n-k+i\\) with\n\\(i\\ge1\\) and using the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\ni+b\\le k+1\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad (i,b)\\in T_k,\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n\\[\nT_k:=\\{(i,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid i+b\\le k+1\\}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus the uncovered set is exactly \\(T_k\\).  A simple count gives  \n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Covering \\(T_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\nWe prove by induction on \\(k\\) that the set \\(T_k\\) can be covered by\n\\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n*Base case \\(k=0\\).*  \\(T_0=\\varnothing\\); no line is needed.\n\n*Base case \\(k=1\\).*  \\(T_1=\\{(1,1)\\}\\).  The line  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;y=2x-1\n\\]\n\nhas slope \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) and passes through \\((1,1)\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume that for some \\(k\\ge2\\) the set \\(T_{k-1}\\) is covered by\n\\(k-1\\) sunny lines.  Set  \n\n\\[\nc:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(2c\\le k+1\\), the point \\((c,c)\\) belongs to \\(T_{k-1}\\).\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL_k:=\\text{the line through }(1,k)\\text{ and }(c,c). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIts slope is  \n\n\\[\nm_k=\\frac{c-k}{c-1}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(c\\in\\{(k-1)/2,\\,(k+1)/2\\}\\) we have \\(2c\\neq k+1\\); consequently\n\\(m_k\\neq-1\\).  Moreover \\(c\\neq1\\) and \\(c\\neq k\\), so\n\\(m_k\\neq0,\\infty\\).  Hence \\(L_k\\) is sunny.\n\nThe line \\(L_k\\) contains the new boundary point \\((1,k)\\) of \\(T_k\\)\nand also the interior point \\((c,c)\\), which is already covered by the\n\\(k-1\\) sunny lines from the induction hypothesis.  Every other point of\n\\(T_k\\) satisfies \\(i+b\\le k\\); such points belong to \\(T_{k-1}\\) and are\nalready covered.  After adding \\(L_k\\) the whole set \\(T_k\\) is covered\nby exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus, by induction, **for every integer \\(k\\ge0\\) the set \\(T_k\\) can be\ncovered by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the whole configuration\n\nFor the prescribed \\(k\\) we now take\n\n* the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (1) – all non‑sunny;\n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines supplied by the induction in Section 3 that\n  cover \\(T_k\\).\n\nThe total number of lines is \\((n-k)+k=n\\); exactly \\(k\\) of them are\nsunny.  Every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered:\n\n* if its first coordinate \\(a\\le n-k\\) it lies on the vertical line\n  \\(x=a\\);\n* otherwise \\((a,b)=(n-k+i,b)\\) with \\(i+b\\le k+1\\), i.e. \\((i,b)\\in T_k\\);\n  such a point is on one of the sunny lines covering \\(T_k\\).\n\nHence the required configuration exists for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Impossibility of other values\n\nThe definition “exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines’’ forces \\(0\\le k\\le n\\); a\nconfiguration with \\(k\\) outside this interval would contain either a\nnegative number of lines or more than \\(n\\) lines, which is impossible.\nSince we have exhibited a construction for **every** integer in the\ninterval, no other values of \\(k\\) are admissible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**  \nAll non‑negative integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) satisfy the\nrequirements.  The final answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\; } .\n\\]"
