e for every even integer \\(k\\ge2\\) a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines is impossible.\n\n4. **Explicit constructions for the admissible small values.**\n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  All of them are non‑sunny, so the number of sunny lines is \\(0\\).  Every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\) where \\(a\\) is its first coordinate.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and one additional sunny line, for instance  \n\n   \\[\n   L:\\;y=2x-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The vertical lines cover all points with first coordinate \\(\\le n-1\\); the remaining points are exactly the set \\(T_1=\\{(1,1)\\}\\), which lies on \\(L\\).\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  \n   Let the non‑sunny part consist of the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).  \n   The uncovered set is \\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).  \n   The following three sunny lines cover it:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y = x      &&\\text{(slope }1\\text{)} ,\\\\\n   L_2 &: \\text{through }(1,2)\\text{ and }(3,1) &&\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12 ,\\\\\n   L_3 &: \\text{through }(1,3)\\text{ and }(2,1) &&\\text{slope }-2 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   One checks point‑by‑point that each of the six points of \\(T_3\\) lies on one of the three lines, and that none of the three lines has slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).\n\n5. **What remains open.**  \n   For odd integers \\(k\\ge5\\) the counting bound (2) is *tight*:  \n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil=\\frac{k(k+1)}2=|T_k|.\n   \\]\n   Hence the bound does not rule out the existence of a configuration.  \n   A constructive decomposition of the lattice triangle \\(T_k\\) into exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines is known for \\(k=1\\) and \\(k=3\\); a general method for all odd \\(k\\) has not been found here.  Consequently the full classification of admissible \\(k\\) is still incomplete.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Reduction to the triangle \\(T_k\\)\n\nAssume that a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\nTake any \\(n-k\\) of the non‑sunny lines and, without loss of generality, replace them by the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny, and they still cover every point of \\(S_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\nConsequently the points that are not covered by (1) are precisely  \n\n\\[\nT_k:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nthe lattice triangle of “size’’ \\(k\\).  The remaining \\(k\\) lines of the configuration are all sunny and must cover every point of \\(T_k\\).\n\nThus the original problem is equivalent to:\n\n> **Problem \\(*\\).**  For a given non‑negative integer \\(k\\) decide whether the set \\(T_k\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A universal upper bound for a sunny line\n\nLet a sunny line have reduced slope \\(\\dfrac{p}{q}\\) with \\(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and \\(p\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q\\).\nAll integer points on the line are of the form  \n\n\\[\n(x,y)=(x_0+qt,\\;y_0+pt),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z,\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\((x_0,y_0)\\) is a particular integer point on the line.\nInside the triangle \\(T_k\\) we have the inequality  \n\n\\[\nx+y\\le k+1 .\n\\]\n\nUsing (3) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n(x_0+y_0)+(p+q)t\\le k+1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\) (the forbidden values \\(p+q=0\\) and \\(|p+q|=1\\) correspond respectively to slopes \\(-1\\) and \\(0,\\infty\\)), the integer parameter \\(t\\) can take at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1-(x_0+y_0)}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n\\le\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n=\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\ndifferent values.  Hence **any sunny line contains at most \\(\\displaystyle\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\) points of \\(T_k\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Consequence for the whole family of \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nIf we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, the total number of points of \\(T_k\\) that they can cover is bounded by  \n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(|T_k|=\\dfrac{k(k+1)}2\\), (5) forces  \n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\ge\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n\\Longrightarrow\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\ge\\frac{k+1}{2}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nInequality (6) holds **iff \\(k\\) is odd** (or \\(k=0\\)).  Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{If }k\\ge2\\text{ is even, no configuration exists.}}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Constructions for the admissible small values\n\nWe now exhibit explicit families of lines for the three values that are known to satisfy the requirements.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n\\]\n\nAll of them are non‑sunny, so the number of sunny lines is \\(0\\).  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(a\\le n\\); the point lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  Hence every required point is covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  The case \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n\\]\n\ntogether with the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y=2x-1 .\n\\]\n\nThe vertical lines cover all points with first coordinate \\(\\le n-1\\).  \nThe only points that are not yet covered are those with first coordinate \\(n\\); after subtracting \\(n-1\\) from the first coordinate they become the single point \\((1,1)\\), which lies on \\(L\\).  Thus the whole set \\(S_n\\) is covered and exactly one line (namely \\(L\\)) is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.3  The case \\(k=3\\)\n\nLet the non‑sunny part consist of the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-3 .\n\\]\n\nThe uncovered set is  \n\n\\[\nT_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe following three sunny lines cover it:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y = x      &&\\text{slope }1,\\\\\nL_2 &: \\text{through }(1,2)\\text{ and }(3,1) &&\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12 ,\\\\\nL_3 &: \\text{through }(1,3)\\text{ and }(2,1) &&\\text{slope }-2 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nA direct substitution shows that each of the six points of \\(T_3\\) lies on one of the three lines, and that none of the three slopes equals \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  Consequently the whole set \\(S_n\\) is covered by \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly three of which are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  What is still unknown\n\nFor odd integers \\(k\\ge5\\) the counting bound (5) is *tight*:\n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n=\n\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n=|T_k|.\n\\]\n\nHence the bound does **not** forbid a covering of \\(T_k\\) by \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nAt present I have not succeeded in producing a general construction for all such odd \\(k\\), nor have I been able to prove that a covering is impossible.  Determining the status of the remaining odd values therefore remains an open sub‑problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\n*   No configuration exists for any even integer \\(k\\ge2\\).  \n*   Configurations do exist for \\(k=0,\\;k=1\\) and \\(k=3\\).  \n*   The existence for odd \\(k\\ge5\\) is not settled by the arguments presented here.  \n\nThus the set of values of \\(k\\) that are **known** to be admissible is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,3\\}} .\n\\]"
