rizontal or of slope \\(-1\\); they may already cover some points of the translated triangle \\(T_k\\).  After replacement those points become uncovered, and it is not shown that the original sunny lines can be altered (or that new sunny lines exist) to still cover the enlarged uncovered set.  Therefore the step “without loss of generality’’ is not justified.\n\n**Classification:** **Justification Gap** – the reduction to covering \\(T_k\\) by the \\(k\\) sunny lines is not rigorously established.\n\n---\n\n### 3. A universal upper bound for a sunny line  \n\n> “Let a sunny line have reduced slope \\(\\dfrac{p}{q}\\) with \\(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and \\(p\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q\\).  \n> All integer points on the line are of the form  \n> \\[\n> (x,y)=(x_0+qt,\\;y_0+pt),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z,\n> \\tag{3}\n> \\]  \n> where \\((x_0,y_0)\\) is a particular integer point on the line.  \n> Inside the triangle \\(T_k\\) we have the inequality \\(x+y\\le k+1\\).  \n> Using (3) we obtain  \n> \\[\n> (x_0+y_0)+(p+q)t\\le k+1 .\n> \\]  \n> Since \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\) (the forbidden values \\(p+q=0\\) and \\(|p+q|=1\\) correspond respectively to slopes \\(-1\\) and \\(0,\\infty\\)), the integer parameter \\(t\\) can take at most  \n> \\[\n> \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1-(x_0+y_0)}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n> \\le\n> \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n> =\n> \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n> \\tag{4}\n> \\]  \n> different values.  Hence **any sunny line contains at most \\(\\displaystyle\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\) points of \\(T_k\\).**”\n\n**Correct parts.**  \n* The parametrisation (3) of integer points on a line with rational slope is correct.  \n* The inequality \\((x_0+y_0)+(p+q)t\\le k+1\\) follows from substituting the parametrisation into \\(x+y\\le k+1\\).\n\n**Incorrect claim.**  \nThe statement “\\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\)” is **false** for many sunny lines.  For example:\n* Slope \\(-2\\) corresponds to \\((p,q)=(-2,1)\\); here \\(|p+q|=