ssification:** **Correct** (provided the bound were valid).  No new error introduced here.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Constructions for admissible small values  \n\n#### 5.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\n> “Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\). … Every required point is covered.”\n\n**Verification.**  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(a\\le n\\); the line \\(x=a\\) contains it.  All lines are non‑sunny, distinct, and exactly \\(0\\) are sunny.  **Correct.**\n\n#### 5.2  The case \\(k=1\\)\n\n> “Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with the sunny line \\(L:\\;y=2x-1\\). … The only points that are not yet covered are those with first coordinate \\(n\\); after subtracting \\(n-1\\) from the first coordinate they become the single point \\((1,1)\\), which lies on \\(L\\).”\n\n**Verification.**  \nAfter the vertical lines, the uncovered point of \\(S_n\\) is \\((n,1)\\).  The line \\(y=2x-1\\) passes through \\((1,1)\\) but **does not** pass through \\((n,1)\\) (its value at \\(x=n\\) is \\(y=2n-1\\neq1\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\)).  Hence the point \\((n,1)\\) remains uncovered, and the construction fails.  The intended line should be the shifted version \\(y=2x-2n+1\\), not \\(y=2x-1\\).  As written, the argument does not establish the existence of a configuration with exactly one sunny line.\n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** – the provided construction does not satisfy the covering requirement, so the claim that \\(k=1\\) is admissible is unsupported.\n\n#### 5.3  The case \\(k=3\\)\n\n> “Let the non‑sunny part consist of the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\). … The three sunny lines  \n> \\[\n> L_1:y=x,\\qquad\n> L_2:\\text{through }(1,2)\\text{ and }(3,1),\\qquad\n> L_3:\\text{through }(1,3)\\text{ and }(2,1)\n> \\]  \n> cover the six points of \\(T_3\\).”\n\n**Verification.**  \nA direct check shows each of the six points \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\) lies on one of the three lines, and none of th