 in the *same* order.\n   Consequently two distinct lines of the same slope would have to contain the\n   *same* point on the antidiagonal \\(x+y=3\\), which is impossible because the\n   lines are required to be distinct.  Hence a covering with \\(k\\ge5\\) odd\n   cannot exist.\n\n6. **Existence for the remaining values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  All are\n   non‑sunny and every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together\n   with the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L:\\;y=2x-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The vertical lines cover all points with first coordinate \\(\\le n-1\\); the\n   remaining points are exactly \\((n,1)\\), and \\((n,1)\\) satisfies\n   \\(1=2n-1\\) only when \\(n=1\\), which is impossible.  To correct this we\n   replace the sunny line by  \n\n   \\[\n   L:\\;y=-(n-2)x+(n-1) .\n   \\]\n\n   For any \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   L(a)=-(n-2)a+(n-1)=n-a .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the point \\((a,n-a)\\) (which belongs to \\(S_n\\) because\n   \\(a+(n-a)=n\\le n+1\\)) lies on \\(L\\).  All points with first coordinate\n   \\(a\\le n-1\\) are already covered by the vertical line \\(x=a\\); the point\n   \\((n,1)\\) is covered by \\(L\\) because \\(L(n)=1\\).  Thus every required\n   point is covered and exactly one line (namely \\(L\\)) is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  Use the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).  The\n   uncovered set is  \n\n   \\[\n   T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y = x ,\\\\\n   L_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12\\,x + \\tfrac32 ,\\\\\n   L_3 &: y = -2x + 5\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   contain respectively  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c}\n   \\text{point} & \\text{line that contains it}\\\\ \\hline\n   (1,1) & L_1\\\\\n   (2,2) & L_1\\\\\n   (1,2) & L_2\\\\\n   (3,1) & L_2\\\\\n   (1,3) & L_3\\\\\n   (2,1) & L_3\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   (each verification is a direct substitution, e.g. \\(1=-\\tfrac12\\cdot3+\\tfrac32\\)).  \n   Hence all six points of \\(T_3\\) are covered and the three lines are sunny.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the impossibility results of Sections 3 and 5 with the explicit\n   constructions of Section 6 we obtain that the only non‑negative integers\n   \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists are  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}.\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Reduction to the triangle \\(T_k\\)\n\nAssume that for a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines,\nexactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, and that every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least\none of the lines.\n\nAmong the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines we may replace each of them by a vertical\nline (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis) that passes through the same set of points\nof \\(S_n\\); this replacement does not destroy the covering property because\nvertical lines are also non‑sunny.  After the replacement we have the\n\\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) whose first coordinate is at most \\(n-k\\) are already\ncovered.  The points that remain uncovered are precisely\n\n\\[\nT_k:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the original problem is equivalent to the following.\n\n> **Problem \\(*\\).**  For a given non‑negative integer \\(k\\) decide whether the\n> set \\(T_k\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Maximum number of points of \\(T_k\\) on a single sunny line\n\nLet a sunny line have reduced rational slope \\(\\dfrac{p}{q}\\) with\n\\(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and \\(p\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q\\).\nAll integer points on the line are described by  \n\n\\[\n(x,y)=(x_0+qt,\\;y_0+pt),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAdding the coordinates gives  \n\n\\[\nx+y=(x_0+y_0)+(p+q)t .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nInside the triangle \\(T_k\\) we have \\(2\\le x+y\\le k+1\\).  Hence the integer\nparameter \\(t\\) can assume at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1-(x_0+y_0)}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n\\le\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\ndifferent values.  Therefore a sunny line contains at most  \n\n\\[\ng_k(p,q):=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_k\\).\n\nThe function \\(g_k(p,q)\\) is maximised when \\(|p+q|\\) is as small as possible.\nBecause the line is sunny we have \\(|p+q|\\neq0,1\\) only when the slope is\n\\(-1\\) (for \\(|p+q|=0\\)) or horizontal/vertical (for \\(|p+q|=1\\)).  Hence\nthe smallest admissible value is \\(|p+q|=2\\), and\n\n\\[\n\\max_{(p,q)} g_k(p,q)=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n=\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus **any sunny line meets at most \\(\\displaystyle\n\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\) points of \\(T_k\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Counting the whole family of \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nIf we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, the total number of points of \\(T_k\\)\nthat they can cover is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\;\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\dfrac{k(k+2)}2, & k\\text{ even},\\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{k(k+1)}2, & k\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(|T_k|=\\dfrac{k(k+1)}2\\), (8) yields two consequences.\n\n*If \\(k\\) is even and \\(k\\ge2\\)*, then  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{k(k+2)}2>\\frac{k(k+1)}2,\n\\]\n\nso the bound is **strictly smaller** than the required number of points;\nhence a covering is impossible.  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{no even }k\\ge2\\text{ works}.}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(k\\) is odd*, the bound in (8) coincides with \\(|T_k|\\).  Therefore a\ncovering can exist only if **every** sunny line attains the maximal\n\\(\\displaystyle\\frac{k+1}{2}\\) points.  By (7) this forces \\(|p+q|=2\\) for\neach line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Sunny lines with \\(|p+q|=2\\)\n\nThe Diophantine condition \\(|p+q|=2\\) together with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and\n\\(p\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q\\) has exactly three solutions:\n\n\\[\n(p,q)=(1,1),\\;(-2,1),\\;(-1,2),\n\\]\n\nwhich correspond to the three slopes  \n\n\\[\ns_1=1,\\qquad s_2=-2,\\qquad s_3=-\\tfrac12 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nA line of slope \\(1\\) meets the antidiagonals \\(x+y=2,4,6,\\dots\\); a line\nof slope \\(-2\\) or \\(-\\tfrac12\\) meets the antidiagonals\n\\(x+y=3,5,7,\\dots\\).  Hence each of the three families of lines contains\nexactly \\(\\dfrac{k+1}{2}\\) points of \\(T_k\\), all lying on antidiagonals of the\nsame parity.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Impossibility for odd \\(k\\ge5\\)\n\nIn the triangle \\(T_k\\) the numbers of points on the even‑parity antidiagonals\n\\(x+y=2,4,\\dots ,k+1\\) are  \n\n\\[\n1,3,5,\\dots ,k\\quad\\bigl(\\tfrac{k+1}{2}\\text{ numbers}\\bigr),\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nand on the odd‑parity antidiagonals \\(x+y=3,5,\\dots ,k\\) they are  \n\n\\[\n2,4,6,\\dots ,k-1\\quad\\bigl(\\tfrac{k-1}{2}\\text{ numbers}\\bigr).\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nA line of slope 1 can cover only even‑parity antidiagonals, therefore the\ntotal number of points contributed by all slope‑1 lines equals the sum in\n(11).  Since each such line contributes \\(\\dfrac{k+1}{2}\\) points, the number\n\\(m\\) of slope‑1 lines must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nm\\cdot\\frac{k+1}{2}=1+3+5+\\dots +k\n=\\Bigl(\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr)^2 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(m=\\dfrac{k+1}{2}\\).  Because we have only \\(k\\) lines in total,\nthe remaining \\(k-m=\\dfrac{k-1}{2}\\) lines must be of slope \\(-2\\) or\n\\(-\\tfrac12\\).\n\nEach of those remaining lines also contributes \\(\\dfrac{k+1}{2}\\) points, so\nthey together cover  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{k-1}{2}\\cdot\\frac{k+1}{2}\n=2+4+6+\\dots +(k-1),\n\\]\n\nexactly the total number of points on the odd‑parity antidiagonals (12).  Hence\n**every** remaining line must meet *all* odd‑parity antidiagonals.\n\nBut a line of slope \\(-2\\) meets the odd‑parity antidiagonals in the order\n\\(x+y=3,5,7,\\dots\\); the same is true for a line of slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\).\nConsequently two distinct lines of the same slope would have to share the\nunique point on the antidiagonal \\(x+y=3\\), contradicting the requirement that\nthe \\(k\\) lines be distinct.  Therefore a covering with odd \\(k\\ge5\\) cannot\nexist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Constructions for the admissible values\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  All are non‑sunny and\n  each point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with the\n  sunny line  \n\n  \\[\n  L:\\;y=-(n-2)x+(n-1).\n  \\]\n\n  For any \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) we have \\(L(a)=n-a\\); thus the point\n  \\((a,n-a)\\in S_n\\) lies on \\(L\\).  All points with first coordinate\n  \\(a\\le n-1\\) are already covered by the vertical line \\(x=a\\); the point\n  \\((n,1)\\) is covered by \\(L\\) because \\(L(n)=1\\).  Hence every required\n  point is covered and exactly one line (namely \\(L\\)) is sunny.\n\n* **\\(k=3\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).  The uncovered set is\n  \\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).  The three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L_1 &: y = x ,\\\\\n  L_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12\\,x + \\tfrac32 ,\\\\\n  L_3 &: y = -2x + 5\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  contain respectively  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c}\n  \\text{point} & \\text{line that contains it}\\\\ \\hline\n  (1,1) & L_1\\\\\n  (2,2) & L_1\\\\\n  (1,2) & L_2\\\\\n  (3,1) & L_2\\\\\n  (1,3) & L_3\\\\\n  (2,1) & L_3\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n\n  (each verification is a direct substitution, e.g. \\(1=-\\tfrac12\\cdot3+\\tfrac32\\)).\n  Thus all six points of \\(T_3\\) are covered and the three lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7. Final conclusion\n\nThe impossibility results of Sections 3 and 5 together with the explicit\nconstructions of Section 6 show that a configuration satisfying the two\nrequirements exists **iff**  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,3\\}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore the answer to the original problem is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0,\\;k=1,\\;k=3\\; } .\n\\]"
