e 1 can cover only even‑parity antidiagonals… Consequently two distinct lines of the same slope would have to share the unique point on the antidiagonal \\(x+y=3\\)…”\n  * **Issue:** **Justification Gap** – A slope‑1 line can also cover odd‑parity antidiagonals (by choosing an odd intercept). The parity argument is incomplete and contains inaccuracies.\n* **Location:** “Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with the sunny line \\(L:\\;y=-(n-2)x+(n-1)\\). … the point \\((n,1)\\) is covered by \\(L\\) because \\(L(n)=1\\).”\n  * **Issue:** **Critical Error** – The line \\(y=-(n-2)x+(n-1)\\) does **not** pass through \\((n,1)\\) (e.g. for \\(n=3\\) it gives \\(y=-1\\)). Hence the construction fails to cover all required points.\n* **Location:** “The three sunny lines  \n  \\(L_1: y = x,\\; L_2: y = -\\tfrac12\\,x + \\tfrac32,\\; L_3: y = -2x + 5\\) contain respectively … \\((1,2)\\) on \\(L_2\\), \\((3,1)\\) on \\(L_2\\).”\n  * **Issue:** **Critical Error** – The equation for \\(L_2\\) is incorrect; \\(y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32\\) does **not** contain \\((1,2)\\) or \\((3,1)\\). The claimed covering of \\(T_3\\) is therefore invalid.\n* **Location:** “Thus the impossibility results of Sections 3 and 5 together with the explicit constructions of Section 6 show that a configuration … exists **iff** \\(k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\).”\n  * **Issue:** **Justification Gap** – The final conclusion rests on the flawed arguments and constructions above; the proof is incomplete despite the answer being correct.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Verification Log**\n\n---\n\n### 1. Definition of \\(S_n\\) and its size  \n\n> “Let  \n> \\[\n> S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n> \\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n> \\]”\n\n*Correct.*  The set \\(S_n\\) is precisely the integer points in the right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(1,n),(n,1)\\); counting gives \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Reduction to the triangle \\(T_k\\)\n\n> “Assume that for a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, and that every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines.  \n> Among the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines we may replace each of them by a vertical line (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis) that passes through the same set of points of \\(S_n\\); this replacement does not destroy the covering property because vertical lines are also non‑sunny. After the replacement we have the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n> \\[\n> x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n> \\tag{1}\n> \\]”\n\n*Critical Error.*  A non‑sunny line may be horizontal (\\(y=c\\)) or have slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).  Such a line contains points with **varying** \\(x\\)-coordinates (for a horizontal line) or with **varying** \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) that satisfy \\(x+y=c\\).  Replacing it by a vertical line \\(x=a\\) can at best preserve a **single** point of intersection with \\(S_n\\); in general the vertical line does **not** pass through the same set of points.  Consequently the covering property can be destroyed, and the claimed equivalence to a problem involving only vertical lines is false.\n\n> “Consequently the original problem is equivalent to the following.  \n> **Problem \\(*\\).**  For a given non‑negative integer \\(k\\) decide whether the set \\(T_k\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.”\n\n*Justification Gap.*  The equivalence is asserted based on the (invalid) replacement above; no rigorous argument is provided that the set of uncovered points after the replacement is exactly \\(T_k\\) nor that distinctness of lines is preserved.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Maximum number of points of \\(T_k\\) on a single sunny line  \n\n> “Let a sunny line have reduced rational slope \\(\\dfrac{p}{q}\\) with \\(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and \\(p\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q\\).  \n> All integer points on the line are described by  \n> \\[\n> (x,y)=(x_0+qt,\\;y_0+pt),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n> \\tag{3}\n> \\]  \n> Adding the coordinates gives  \n> \\[\n> x+y=(x_0+y_0)+(p+q)t .\n> \\tag{4}\n> \\]  \n> Inside the triangle \\(T_k\\) we have \\(2\\le x+y\\le k+1\\).  Hence the integer parameter \\(t\\) can assume at most  \n> \\[\n> \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1-(x_0+y_0)}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n> \\le\n> \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n> \\tag{5}\n> \\]  \n> different values.  Therefore a sunny line contains at most  \n> \\[\n> g_k(p,q):=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n> \\tag{6}\n> \\]  \n> points of \\(T_k\\).”\n\n*Correct derivation* of the bound \\(g_k(p,q)\\) for a line with rational slope \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\) that passes through at least one integer point of \\(T_k\\).\n\n> “The function \\(g_k(p,q)\\) is maximised when \\(|p+q|\\) is as small as possible. Because the line is sunny we have \\(|p+q|\\neq0,1\\) only when the slope is \\(-1\\) (for \\(|p+q|=0\\)) or horizontal/vertical (for \\(|p+q|=1\\)).  Hence the smallest admissible value is \\(|p+q|=2\\), and  \n> \\[\n> \\max_{(p,q)} g_k(p,q)=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n> =\n> \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n> \\tag{7}\n> \\]”\n\n*Justification Gap / Factual Error.*  Sunny lines with \\(|p+q|=1\\) **do exist**: for example the line of slope \\(-2\\) has \\((p,q)=(-2,1)\\) giving \\(|p+q|=|-2+1|=1\\); similarly slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\) has \\((p,q)=(-1,2)\\) with \\(|p+q|=1\\).  The argument incorrectly excludes these slopes, claiming that \\(|p+q|=1\\) corresponds only to horizontal or vertical lines.  Consequently the statement “the smallest admissible value is \\(|p+q|=2\\)” is false.  Nevertheless the final bound \\(\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\) happens to be correct (it is attained by slopes \\(\\pm2\\) and \\(\\pm\\frac12\\)), but the justification is incomplete.\n\n> “Thus **any sunny line meets at most \\(\\displaystyle\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\) points of \\(T_k\\).**”\n\n*Correct conclusion*, but the reasoning leading to it contains the gap identified above.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Counting the whole family of \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\n> “If we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, the total number of points of \\(T_k\\) that they can cover is at most  \n> \\[\n> k\\;\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil=\n> \\begin{cases}\n> \\dfrac{k(k+2)}2, & k\\text{ even},\\\\[6pt]\n> \\dfrac{k(k+1)}2, & k\\text{ odd}.\n> \\end{cases}\n> \\tag{8}\n> \\]”\n\n*Correct algebraic manipulation* of the bound from (7).\n\n> “Because \\(|T_k|=\\dfrac{k(k+1)}2\\), (8) yields two consequences.  \n> *If \\(k\\) is even and \\(k\\ge2\\), then  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{k(k+2)}2>\\frac{k(k+1)}2,\n> \\]  \n> so the bound is **strictly smaller** than the required number of points; hence a covering is impossible.  Consequently  \n> \\[\n> \\boxed{\\text{no even }k\\ge2\\text{ works}.}\n> \\tag{9}\n> \\]”\n\n*Critical Error.*  The inequality direction is reversed: \\(\\frac{k(k+2)}2\\) is **greater** than \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\).  Since the bound is an **upper** bound on the number of points that can be covered, a larger bound does **not** preclude covering all points.  Therefore the impossibility claim for even \\(k\\) is not justified by the argument presented.\n\n> “*If \\(k\\) is odd*, the bound in (8) coincides with \\(|T_k|\\).  Therefore a covering can exist only if **every** sunny line attains the maximal \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{k+1}{2}\\) points.  By (7) this forces \\(|p+q|=2\\) for each line.”\n\n*Correct observation* that for odd \\(k\\) the bound matches \\(|T_k|\\); however the statement “forces \\(|p+q|=2\\)” relies on the earlier (incorrect) claim that \\(|p+q|=2\\) is the only way to achieve the maximum, ignoring the admissible \\(|p+q|=1\\) cases (slopes \\(-2\\) and \\(-\\tfrac12\\)).  The conclusion is therefore based on a flawed premise.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Sunny lines with \\(|p+q|=2\\)\n\n> “The Diophantine condition \\(|p+q|=2\\) together with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and \\(p\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q\\) has exactly three solutions:  \n> \\[\n> (p,q)=(1,1),\\;(-2,1),\\;(-1,2),\n> \\]  \n> which correspond to the three slopes  \n> \\[\n> s_1=1,\\qquad s_2=-2,\\qquad s_3=-\\tfrac12 .\n> \\tag{10}\n> \\]”\n\n*Correct classification* of the integer pairs with \\(|p+q|=2\\).  (Note that the earlier claim that \\(|p+q|=2\\) is the *only* way to achieve the maximum is not justified, but the enumeration itself is accurate.)\n\n> “A line of slope 1 meets the antidiagonals \\(x+y=2,4,6,\\dots\\); a line of slope \\(-2\\) or \\(-\\tfrac12\\) meets the antidiagonals \\(x+y=3,5,7,\\dots\\).  Hence each of the three families of lines contains exactly \\(\\dfrac{k+1}{2}\\) points of \\(T_k\\), all lying on antidiagonals of the same parity.”\n\n*Correct description* of how the three slopes intersect the antidiagonals; this part is sound.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Impossibility for odd \\(k\\ge5\\)\n\n> “In the triangle \\(T_k\\) the numbers of points on the even‑parity antidiagonals \\(x+y=2,4,\\dots ,k+1\\) are … (11) … and on the odd‑parity antidiagonals … (12).  \n> A line of slope 1 can cover only even‑parity antidiagonals, therefore the total number of points contributed by all slope‑1 lines equals the sum in (11). … Consequently two distinct lines of the same slope would have to share the unique point on the antidiagonal \\(x+y=3\\), contradicting the requirement that the \\(k\\) lines be distinct.  Therefore a covering with odd \\(k\\ge5\\) cannot exist.”\n\n*Justification Gap.*  The statement “a line of slope 1 can cover only even‑parity antidiagonals” is false: a slope‑1 line with an odd intercept covers odd‑parity antidiagonals.  Moreover, the argument that two lines of the same slope must share the point on \\(x+y=3\\) assumes that every line of slope \\(-2\\) or \\(-\\tfrac12\\) must intersect **all** odd‑parity antidiagonals, which is not true (e.g. a line of slope \\(-2\\) cannot pass through both the antidiagonals \\(x+y=3\\) and \\(x+y=5\\) with integer points).  Hence the parity‑based contradiction is not rigorously established.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Constructions for the admissible values  \n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  “Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  All are non‑sunny and each point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).”\n\n  *Correct* construction.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  “Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with the sunny line  \n  \\[\n  L:\\;y=-(n-2)x+(n-1).\n  \\]  \n  For any \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) we have \\(L(a)=n-a\\); thus the point \\((a,n-a)\\in S_n\\) lies on \\(L\\). … the point \\((n,1)\\) is covered by \\(L\\) because \\(L(n)=1\\).”\n\n  *Critical Error.*  Substituting \\(x=n\\) gives \\(y=-(n-2)n+(n-1)= -n^{2}+3n-1\\), which equals \\(1\\) only for \\(n=2\\) (outside the allowed range).  For \\(n\\ge3\\) the line does **not** pass through \\((n,1)\\); consequently the construction fails to cover all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n* **\\(k=3\\).**  \n  “Use the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).  The uncovered set is  \n  \\[\n  T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n  \\]  \n  The three sunny lines  \n  \\[\n  L_1 : y = x ,\\qquad\n  L_2 : y = -\\tfrac12\\,x + \\tfrac32 ,\\qquad\n  L_3 : y = -2x + 5\n  \\]  \n  contain respectively … (each verification is a direct substitution, e.g. \\(1=-\\tfrac12\\cdot3+\\tfrac32\\)).”\n\n  *Critical Error.*  The line \\(L_2\\) is mis‑written.  The correct line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) is \\(y=-\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52\\).  With the given equation \\(y=-\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac32\\) we have \\(y(1)=2\\) false and \\(y(3)=1\\) false; the claimed points are not on the line.  Hence the construction does **not** cover \\(T_3\\) as stated.\n\n---\n\n### 8. Final conclusion  \n\n> “The impossibility results of Sections 3 and 5 together with the explicit constructions of Section 6 show that a configuration … exists **iff** \\(k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\).”\n\n*Justification Gap.*  The conclusion rests on the flawed reduction (Section 1), the incorrect inequality for even \\(k\\) (Section 3), the incomplete parity argument (Section 5), and the invalid constructions for \\(k=1\\) and \\(k=3\\) (Section 6).  Although the final answer happens to be correct, the proof does not rigorously establish it.  \n\n---"
