lope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Reduction to the triangle \\(T_k\\)\n\nLet a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfy the two conditions and let\nexactly \\(k\\) of them be sunny.  Put \\(m=n-k\\) (the number of non‑sunny\nlines).  Because a non‑sunny line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis,\nthe \\(y\\)-axis or to the line \\(x+y=0\\), we may replace each of them by a\nvertical line without creating a sunny line:\n\n* a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) can be rotated by \\(90^{\\circ}\\) (swap the\n  coordinates) – the set of points to be covered is unchanged and the line\n  becomes vertical;\n* a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=d\\).  All points of\n  \\(S_n\\) on this line satisfy \\(d\\le n+1\\); among them choose the one with\n  smallest \\(x\\)-coordinate, say \\((a,b)\\).  The vertical line \\(x=a\\) passes\n  through that point and is non‑sunny.  Repeating this for every non‑sunny\n  line we obtain \\(m\\) distinct vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\) (the\n  distinctness follows because the chosen \\(a\\)’s are different – each\n  vertical line contains at least one point that was covered only by the\n  original line).\n\nThus we may **assume** that the non‑sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m\\qquad(m=n-k).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) with first coordinate \\(a\\le m\\) are now covered.\nA point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(a>m\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\na\\ge m+1,\\qquad b\\le n+1-a\\le (m+k+1)-(m+1)=k .\n\\]\n\nDefine \\(a'=a-m\\;(1\\le a'\\le k)\\); then \\(a'+b\\le k+1\\).  Hence the set of\nstill uncovered points is precisely  \n\n\\[\nT_k:=\\{(a',b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a'+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the original problem is equivalent to:\n\n> **Problem \\(*\\).**  For a given non‑negative integer \\(k\\) decide whether the\n> set \\(T_k\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. How many points of \\(T_k\\) can lie on one sunny line?\n\nLet a sunny line have reduced rational slope \\(\\dfrac{p}{q}\\) with\n\\(q>0,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and \\(p\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q\\) (the excluded cases give\nhorizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\)).  All integer points on the line are\n\n\\[\n(x,y)=(x_0+qt,\\;y_0+pt),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAdding the coordinates yields  \n\n\\[\nx+y=(x_0+y_0)+(p+q)t .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nInside \\(T_k\\) we have \\(2\\le x+y\\le k+1\\).  Hence the integer parameter\n\\(t\\) can take at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1-(x_0+y_0)}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n\\le\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\ndifferent values.  Therefore a sunny line contains at most  \n\n\\[\ng_k(p,q):=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_k\\).\n\nBecause the line is sunny we have \\(|p+q|\\neq0\\) (otherwise the slope would be\n\\(-1\\)).  Moreover \\(|p+q|=1\\) would give a slope \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\), also\nforbidden.  Hence \\(|p+q|\\ge2\\).  From (6) we obtain the universal bound  \n\n\\[\ng_k(p,q)\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus **any sunny line meets at most**\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil}\n\\]\npoints of \\(T_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Counting the whole family of \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nIf we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, the total number of points of \\(T_k\\) they\ncan cover is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\dfrac{k(k+2)}2, & k\\text{ even},\\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{k(k+1)}2, & k\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|T_k|=\\dfrac{k(k+1)}2\\),\n\n* for **even** \\(k\\ge2\\) the bound in (8) is *strictly larger* than the\n  required number of points, but equality can be attained only when every\n  line reaches the maximal size \\(\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\).\n  This forces \\(|p+q|=2\\) for each line (see §4).  We shall see that the\n  parity of the antidiagonals then makes a covering impossible, so no even\n  \\(k\\ge2\\) works.\n\n* for **odd** \\(k\\) the bound in (8) equals \\(|T_k|\\); therefore a covering\n  is possible only if *every* sunny line attains the maximal size\n  \\(\\dfrac{k+1}{2}\\).  Again this forces \\(|p+q|=2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Sunny lines that attain the maximal size\n\nThe Diophantine condition \\(|p+q|=2\\) together with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) yields\nexactly three possibilities:\n\n\\[\n(p,q)=(1,1),\\;(-2,1),\\;(-1,2)\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\text{slope }s\\in\\Bigl\\{1,\\,-2,\\,-\\tfrac12\\Bigr\\}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n* A line of slope \\(1\\) has equation \\(y=x+c\\).  Substituting into\n  \\(x+y=s\\) gives \\(s=2x+c\\); hence it meets only antidiagonals of the same\n  parity as \\(c\\) (all even antidiagonals if \\(c\\) is even, all odd if \\(c\\) is\n  odd).  By choosing \\(c\\) appropriately we obtain a line that meets **every**\n  even antidiagonal of \\(T_k\\) and contains exactly \\(\\dfrac{k+1}{2}\\) points.\n\n* A line of slope \\(-2\\) (equation \\(y=-2x+c\\)) or of slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)\n  (\\(y=-\\tfrac12x+c\\)) meets only antidiagonals of the opposite parity.\n  With a suitable intercept each such line meets **every** odd antidiagonal\n  and also contains \\(\\dfrac{k+1}{2}\\) points.\n\nThus any covering that uses the maximal number of points per line must consist\nsolely of lines of the three slopes listed in (9), with the slope‑\\(1\\) lines\ncovering the even antidiagonals and the slope \\(-2\\) / \\(-\\tfrac12\\) lines\ncovering the odd antidiagonals.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Parity obstruction for \\(k\\ge5\\)\n\nWrite \\(k=2t+1\\) with \\(t\\ge2\\) (the case \\(k\\) even is handled later).\nThe antidiagonals of \\(T_k\\) are \\(x+y=2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\).\n\n*Even antidiagonals.*  \nFor \\(s=2,4,\\dots ,k+1\\) the number of points on the antidiagonal is\n\\(s-1\\), i.e. \\(1,3,5,\\dots ,k\\).  Their total is  \n\n\\[\nE:=1+3+5+\\dots +k=\\Bigl(\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr)^2=(t+1)^2 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n*Odd antidiagonals.*  \nFor \\(s=3,5,\\dots ,k\\) the numbers are \\(2,4,6,\\dots ,k-1\\); their total is  \n\n\\[\nO:=2+4+6+\\dots +(k-1)=\\frac{k-1}{2}\\cdot\\frac{k+1}{2}=t(t+1) .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nA slope‑\\(1\\) line can meet only even antidiagonals, and each such line\ncontributes exactly \\(\\dfrac{k+1}{2}=t+1\\) points.  Hence the number of\nslope‑\\(1\\) lines must be  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{E}{t+1}= \\frac{(t+1)^2}{t+1}=t+1 .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThe remaining \\(k-(t+1)=t\\) lines must have slope \\(-2\\) or \\(-\\tfrac12\\); each\ncontributes again \\(t+1\\) points, so together they supply  \n\n\\[\nt(t+1)=O\n\\]\n\npoints, exactly the amount needed on the odd antidiagonals.\n\nHowever, a line of slope \\(-2\\) (or of slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)) meets **every**\nodd antidiagonal exactly once.  Consequently two distinct lines of the *same*\nslope would both contain the unique point on the antidiagonal \\(x+y=3\\),\ncontradicting the requirement that the \\(k\\) lines be distinct.  Therefore\namong the \\(t\\) remaining lines at most one can have slope \\(-2\\) and at most\none can have slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\).  This forces \\(t\\le1\\), i.e. \\(k\\le3\\).\n\nThus **no odd \\(k\\ge5\\) admits a covering**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Even \\(k\\ge2\\) are impossible\n\nLet \\(k=2t\\) with \\(t\\ge1\\).  The numbers of points on even and odd\nantidiagonals are, respectively,\n\n\\[\nE'=1+3+\\dots +(2t-1)=t^{2},\\qquad\nO'=2+4+\\dots +2t=t(t+1).\n\\]\n\nA slope‑\\(1\\) line can only contribute to \\(E'\\); each such line gives\n\\(t+1\\) points, so the number of slope‑\\(1\\) lines would have to be\n\\(E'/(t+1)=t^{2}/(t+1)\\), which is not an integer for any \\(t\\ge1\\).\nHence the parity distribution cannot be matched, and **no even \\(k\\ge2\\) works**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7. Constructions for the admissible values\n\n*\\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n\\]\n\nAll are non‑sunny; each point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).\n\n*\\(k=1\\).*  \nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n\\]\n\nand the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\; y = 2x-2n+1 .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(a\\le n-1\\) the point \\((a,b)\\) is covered by the vertical line\n\\(x=a\\).  The only point not covered by those vertical lines is \\((n,1)\\);\nindeed \\(L(n)=2n-2n+1=1\\), so \\((n,1)\\) lies on \\(L\\).  Thus every required\npoint is covered and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n*\\(k=3\\).*  \nUse the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-3 .\n\\]\n\nThe uncovered set is  \n\n\\[\nT_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y = x ,\\\\\nL_2 &: y = -2x+5 ,\\\\\nL_3 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\ncontain respectively  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{point} & \\text{line that contains it}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1) & L_1\\\\\n(2,2) & L_1\\\\\n(1,2) & L_3\\\\\n(3,1) & L_3\\\\\n(1,3) & L_2\\\\\n(2,1) & L_2\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n(verification: \\(1= -2\\cdot1+5\\), \\(1= -2\\cdot2+5\\), \\(2=-\\tfrac12\\cdot1+\\tfrac52\\),\n\\(1=-\\tfrac12\\cdot3+\\tfrac52\\)).  Hence all six points of \\(T_3\\) are covered\nand the three lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8. Final conclusion\n\nThe impossibility arguments for even \\(k\\ge2\\) (parity mismatch) and for odd\n\\(k\\ge5\\) (necessity of more than one line of the same maximal slope) together\nwith the explicit constructions for \\(k=0,1,3\\) prove that a configuration\nsatisfying the two conditions exists **iff**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\; } .\n\\]"
