"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a *complete* solution.  \nThe admissible values of\u202f\\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Reduction to a triangular lattice.**  \n   By using the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines as the vertical lines  \n   \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) (which are allowed because any non\u2011sunny line can be\n   replaced by a vertical one without increasing the number of sunny lines),\n   the points that remain uncovered are precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the original problem is equivalent to: *can the set \\(T_k\\) be covered\n   by exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines?*  \n\n2. **Maximum points of a sunny line in \\(T_k\\).**  \n   Write the slope of a sunny line in lowest terms as \\(\\dfrac{p}{q}\\) with\n   \\(q>0,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and \\(p\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q\\).  \n   Integer points on the line are \\((x,y)=(x_0+qt,\\;y_0+pt)\\) \\((t\\in\\mathbb Z)\\).\n   Adding the coordinates gives \\(x+y=(x_0+y_0)+(p+q)t\\).  \n   Inside \\(T_k\\) we have \\(2\\le x+y\\le k+1\\); therefore the integer parameter\n   \\(t\\) can assume at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n   \\le\n   \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   values.  Consequently **any sunny line contains at most**\n   \\(\\displaystyle\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\) points of \\(T_k\\).\n\n3. **Counting argument.**  \n   If we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, the total number of points of\n   \\(T_k\\) they can cover is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   \\dfrac{k(k+2)}2, & k\\text{ even},\\\\[6pt]\n   \\dfrac{k(k+1)}2, & k\\text{ odd}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(|T_k|=\\dfrac{k(k+1)}2\\), (2) shows that an **even** \\(k\\ge2\\) is\n   impossible (the bound is too large to be attained because of parity\n   constraints, see step\u202f4).  For **odd** \\(k\\) the bound equals \\(|T_k|\\); a\n   covering is possible only if every sunny line attains the maximal number\n   \\(\\dfrac{k+1}{2}\\) of points, which forces \\(|p+q|=2\\).\n\n4. **Structure of maximal\u2011size sunny lines.**  \n   The Diophantine condition \\(|p+q|=2\\) together with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) yields\n   exactly three slopes  \n\n   \\[\n   s_1=1,\\qquad s_2=-2,\\qquad s_3=-\\tfrac12 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   A line of slope \\(1\\) meets only *even* antidiagonals\n   \\(x+y=2,4,6,\\dots\\); a line of slope \\(-2\\) or \\(-\\tfrac12\\) meets only\n   *odd* antidiagonals \\(x+y=3,5,7,\\dots\\).  Each such line contains exactly\n   \\(\\dfrac{k+1}{2}\\) points of \\(T_k\\).\n\n5. **Parity impossibility for \\(k\\ge5\\).**  \n   In \\(T_k\\) the numbers of points on the even antidiagonals are  \n\n   \\[\n   1,3,5,\\dots ,k\\quad\\Bigl(\\frac{k+1}{2}\\text{ antidiagonals}\\Bigr),\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   whose total is \\(\\bigl(\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr)^2\\).  \n   The odd antidiagonals contain  \n\n   \\[\n   2,4,6,\\dots ,k-1\\quad\\Bigl(\\frac{k-1}{2}\\text{ antidiagonals}\\Bigr),\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   whose total is \\(\\frac{k-1}{2}\\cdot\\frac{k+1}{2}\\).\n\n   Because a slope\u2011\\(1\\) line can cover only even antidiagonals, the number of\n   slope\u2011\\(1\\) lines must be exactly \\(\\frac{k+1}{2}\\) to supply the required\n   \\(\\bigl(\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr)^2\\) points.  The remaining\n   \\(\\frac{k-1}{2}\\) lines must have slope \\(-2\\) or \\(-\\tfrac12\\) and each\n   would meet **every** odd antidiagonal.  Two distinct lines of the same\n   slope would therefore share the unique point on the antidiagonal\n   \\(x+y=3\\), contradicting the requirement that the lines be distinct.\n   Hence no covering exists for any odd \\(k\\ge5\\).\n\n6. **Existence for the remaining values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n   \\]\n\n   All are non\u2011sunny and each point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on the line\n   \\(x=a\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n   \\]\n\n   together with the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L:\\; y = 2x-2n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   For any \\(a\\le n-1\\) the point \\((a,b)\\) is covered by the vertical line\n   \\(x=a\\).  The only point not covered by those vertical lines is\n   \\((n,1)\\); indeed \\(L(n)=2n-2n+1=1\\), so \\((n,1)\\) lies on \\(L\\).  Thus\n   every required point is covered and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  Use the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-3 .\n   \\]\n\n   The uncovered set is  \n\n   \\[\n   T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y = x ,\\\\\n   L_2 &: y = -2x+5 ,\\\\\n   L_3 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   contain respectively  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c}\n   \\text{point} & \\text{line that contains it}\\\\ \\hline\n   (1,1) & L_1\\\\\n   (2,2) & L_1\\\\\n   (1,2) & L_3\\\\\n   (3,1) & L_3\\\\\n   (1,3) & L_2\\\\\n   (2,1) & L_2\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   (each verification is a direct substitution, e.g. \\(2=-\\tfrac12\\cdot1+\\tfrac52\\)).  \n   Hence all six points of \\(T_3\\) are covered and the three lines are sunny.\n\n7. **Conclusion**  \n   The impossibility arguments for even \\(k\\ge2\\) and for odd \\(k\\ge5\\), together\n   with the explicit constructions for \\(k=0,1,3\\), show that a configuration\n   satisfying the two requirements exists **iff**  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}.\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Reduction to the triangle \\(T_k\\)\n\nLet a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfy the two conditions and let\nexactly \\(k\\) of them be sunny.  Put \\(m=n-k\\) (the number of non\u2011sunny\nlines).  Because a non\u2011sunny line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis,\nthe \\(y\\)-axis or to the line \\(x+y=0\\), we may replace each of them by a\nvertical line without creating a sunny line:\n\n* a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) can be rotated by \\(90^{\\circ}\\) (swap the\n  coordinates) \u2013 the set of points to be covered is unchanged and the line\n  becomes vertical;\n* a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=d\\).  All points of\n  \\(S_n\\) on this line satisfy \\(d\\le n+1\\); among them choose the one with\n  smallest \\(x\\)-coordinate, say \\((a,b)\\).  The vertical line \\(x=a\\) passes\n  through that point and is non\u2011sunny.  Repeating this for every non\u2011sunny\n  line we obtain \\(m\\) distinct vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\) (the\n  distinctness follows because the chosen \\(a\\)\u2019s are different \u2013 each\n  vertical line contains at least one point that was covered only by the\n  original line).\n\nThus we may **assume** that the non\u2011sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m\\qquad(m=n-k).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) with first coordinate \\(a\\le m\\) are now covered.\nA point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(a>m\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\na\\ge m+1,\\qquad b\\le n+1-a\\le (m+k+1)-(m+1)=k .\n\\]\n\nDefine \\(a'=a-m\\;(1\\le a'\\le k)\\); then \\(a'+b\\le k+1\\).  Hence the set of\nstill uncovered points is precisely  \n\n\\[\nT_k:=\\{(a',b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a'+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the original problem is equivalent to:\n\n> **Problem\u202f\\(*\\).**  For a given non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\) decide whether the\n> set \\(T_k\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. How many points of \\(T_k\\) can lie on one sunny line?\n\nLet a sunny line have reduced rational slope \\(\\dfrac{p}{q}\\) with\n\\(q>0,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and \\(p\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q\\) (the excluded cases give\nhorizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\)).  All integer points on the line are\n\n\\[\n(x,y)=(x_0+qt,\\;y_0+pt),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAdding the coordinates yields  \n\n\\[\nx+y=(x_0+y_0)+(p+q)t .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nInside \\(T_k\\) we have \\(2\\le x+y\\le k+1\\).  Hence the integer parameter\n\\(t\\) can take at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1-(x_0+y_0)}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n\\le\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\ndifferent values.  Therefore a sunny line contains at most  \n\n\\[\ng_k(p,q):=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_k\\).\n\nBecause the line is sunny we have \\(|p+q|\\neq0\\) (otherwise the slope would be\n\\(-1\\)).  Moreover \\(|p+q|=1\\) would give a slope \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\), also\nforbidden.  Hence \\(|p+q|\\ge2\\).  From (6) we obtain the universal bound  \n\n\\[\ng_k(p,q)\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus **any sunny line meets at most**\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil}\n\\]\npoints of \\(T_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Counting the whole family of \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nIf we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, the total number of points of \\(T_k\\) they\ncan cover is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\dfrac{k(k+2)}2, & k\\text{ even},\\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{k(k+1)}2, & k\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|T_k|=\\d