lel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or to the line \\(x+y=0\\), we may replace each of them by a vertical line without creating a sunny line:\n> * a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) can be rotated by \\(90^{\\circ}\\) (swap the coordinates) – the set of points to be covered is unchanged and the line becomes vertical;\n> * a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=d\\). … we obtain \\(m\\) distinct vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\) (the distinctness follows because the chosen \\(a\\)’s are different – each vertical line contains at least one point that was covered only by the original line).”\n\n*Issue – Justification Gap.*  \nThe argument does **not** prove that after the replacement every point of \\(S_n\\) remains covered:\n\n1. Rotating a single horizontal line to a vertical line changes the set of integer points it contains.  The solution does not show that any point that was covered only by the original horizontal line is still covered after the rotation; other lines in the configuration may not compensate.\n\n2. For a line of slope \\(-1\\) the replacement “choose the point with smallest \\(x\\) and draw the vertical line through it” guarantees that the new vertical line passes through **one** point of the original line, but it gives no guarantee that the other points of the original line remain covered.\n\n3. The claim that the resulting vertical lines are exactly \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\) assumes that the chosen \\(x\\)-coordinates are precisely \\(1,2,\\dots ,m\\); this need not hold without an explicit construction.\n\nThus the reduction from an arbitrary configuration to one with vertical non‑sunny lines is not justified.  The rest of the proof assumes this reduction, so the logical chain is broken at this point.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Description of the uncovered set\n\n> “All points of \\(S_n\\) with first coordinate \\(a\\le m\\) are now covered. … Hence the set of still uncovered points is precisely  \n> \\[\n> T_k:=\\{(a',b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a'+b\\le k+1\\},\n> \\qquad |T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n> \\]”\n\n*Correct **provided** the reduction in step 2 is valid.*  The algebraic derivation of \\(T_k\\) from the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\) is accurate.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Integer points on a sunny line\n\n> “Let a sunny line have reduced rational slope \\(\\dfrac{p}{q}\\) with \\(q>0,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and \\(p\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q\\)… All integer points on the line are \\((x_0+qt,\\;y_0+pt)\\), \\(t\\in\\mathbb Z\\).”\n\n*Correct.*  This is the standard parametrisation of integer points on a line with rational slope.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Bounding the number of points of \\(T_k\\) on a sunny line\n\n> “Adding the coordinates yields \\(x+y=(x_0+y_0)+(p+q)t\\). … Hence a sunny line contains at most  \n> \\[\n> g_k(p,q):=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n> \\]”\n\n*Correct derivation of the formula \\(g_k(p,q)\\).*  The inequality \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1-(x_0+y_0)}{|p+q|}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{|p+q|}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) is valid.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Claim that \\(|p+q|\\ge2\\) for sunny lines\n\n> “Because the line is sunny we have \\(|p+q|\\neq0\\) (otherwise the slope would be \\(-1\\)). Moreover \\(|p+q|=1\\) would give a slope \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\), also forbidden. Hence \\(|p+q|\\ge2\\).”\n\n*Critical Error.*  \nThe statement “\\(|p+q|=1\\) would give a slope \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\)” is false.  Counter‑example: \\((p,q)=(-1,2)\\) gives \\(|p+q|=1\\) and slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\), which is sunny.  Therefore the conclusion \\(|p+q|\\ge2\\) is unjustified.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Consequence of the (false) bound\n\n> “Thus any sunny line meets at most \\(\\displaystyle\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\) points of \\(T_k\\).”\n\n*Critical Error (propagated).*  \nThis inequality follows from the erroneous claim \\(|p+q|\\ge2\\).  For a line with \\(|p+q|=1\\) the bound \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{|p+q|}\\bigr\\rfloor+1 = k+1\\) is much larger, so the asserted universal bound \\(\\lceil\\frac{k+1}{2}\\rceil\\) is not justified.\n\n---\n\n### 8. Counting the total number of points covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\n> “If we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, the total number of points of \\(T_k\\) they can cover is at most  \n> \\[\n> k\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil=\n> \\begin{cases}\n> \\dfrac{k(k+2)}2, & k\\text{ even},\\\\[6pt]\n> \\dfrac{k(k+1)}2, & k\\text{ odd}.\n> \\end{cases}\n> \\]”\n\n*Critical Error (depends on step 6).*  \nSince the bound on a single line is not established, the derived total bound is also unsupported.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Consequences for even and odd \\(k\\)\n\n> “For even \\(k\\ge2\\) the bound in (8) is *strictly larger* … This forces \\(|p+q|=2\\) for each line … We shall see that the parity of the antidiagonals then makes a covering impossible, so no even \\(k\\ge2\\) works.\n> For odd \\(k\\) the bound in (8) equals \\(|T_k|\\); therefore a covering is possible only if *every* sunny line attains the maximal size \\(\\dfrac{k+1}{2}\\). Again this forces \\(|p+q|=2\\).”\n\n*Partial Justification Gap.*  \nEven if we accept the (incorrect) bound, the deduction that “maximal size forces \\(|p+q|=2\\)” is correct (because \\(\\lfloor k/|p+q|\\rfloor+1 = (k+1)/2\\) forces \\(|p+q|=2\\)).  However, the argument that this *automatically* leads to a parity obstruction is not fully justified; it relies on later claims that are themselves false.\n\n---\n\n### 10. Classification of slopes with \\(|p+q|= Diophantine condition \\(|p+q|=2\\) together with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) yields exactly three possibilities  \n> \\[\n> (p,q)=(1,1),\\;(-2,1),\\;(-1,2)\n> \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n> \\text{slope }s\\in\\Bigl\\{1,\\,-2,\\,-\\tfrac12\\Bigr\\}.\n> \\]”\n\n*Critical Error.*  \nThere are infinitely many coprime pairs with \\(|p+q|=2\\); e.g. \\((p,q)=(-1,3)\\) gives \\(|p+q|=2\\) and slope \\(-\\tfrac13\\).  The claim that only the three listed slopes are possible is false, so any argument that depends on this exhaustive list is invalid.\n\n---\n\n### 11. Parity properties of the three listed slopes\n\n> “A line of slope 1 … meets only antidiagonals of the same parity as \\(c\\) (all even antidiagonals if \\(c\\) is even, all odd if \\(c\\) is odd).  \n> A line of slope \\(-2\\) … or of slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\) … meets only antidiagonals of the opposite parity.”\n\n*Critical Error (first part is correct, second part is false).*  \nThe description of slope 1 is accurate.  However, a line of slope \\(-2\\) (or \\(-\\tfrac12\\)) **does not** restrict itself to a single parity; because the sum \\(x+y\\) changes by \\(-1\\) (or \\(+1\\)) each step, the line alternates between even and odd antidiagonals.  Hence the statement “meets only antidiagonals of the opposite parity” is false.\n\n---\n\n### 12. Parity obstruction for odd \\(k\\ge5\\)\n\n> “Consequently two distinct lines of the *same* slope would both contain the unique point on the antidiagonal \\(x+y=3\\), contradicting the requirement that the \\(k\\) lines be distinct.  Therefore … \\(k\\le3\\).”\n\n*Critical Error.*  \nThe contradiction relies on the false claim that a slope \\(-2\\) (or \\(-\\tfrac12\\)) line can contain **only** the point on a given odd antidiagonal.  Since such lines actually intersect alternating antidiagonals, two different lines of the same slope can avoid sharing the same point on \\(x+y=3\\).  Hence the parity obstruction is not established.\n\n---\n\n### 13. Parity obstruction for even \\(k\\ge2\\)\n\n> “For even \\(k\\ge2\\) … the number of slope‑1 lines would have to be \\(\\frac{t^{2}}{t+1}\\), which is not an integer for any \\(t\\ge1\\).”\n\n*Critical Error.*  \nThis argument assumes that slope‑1 lines can be used **only** for even antidiagonals, which is not forced; they could equally be employed for odd antidiagonals.  Moreover, the divisibility claim ignores the existence of other slopes (see step 10) that could be used to balance the parity counts.  Consequently the impossibility proof for even \\(k\\) is invalid.\n\n---\n\n### 14. Constructions for the admissible values\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  “Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).” – Correct; all points of \\(S_n\\) lie on the line \\(x=a\\) for their first coordinate \\(a\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  “Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and the sunny line \\(y = 2x-2n+1\\).” – Correct; the vertical lines cover all points with first coordinate \\(\\le n-1\\), and the remaining point \\((n,1)\\) lies on the given sunny line.  The line has slope \\(2\\), which is sunny.\n\n* **\\(k=3\\).**  “Use the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\) and the three sunny lines  \n  \\[\n  y=x,\\qquad y=-2x+5,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52 .\n  \\]  \n  The table verifies that each of the six points of \\(T_3\\) lies on one of these lines.” – Correct; the explicit verification shows that all points of \\(T_3\\) are covered and the three slopes are sunny.\n\nThus the explicit constructions are valid.\n\n---\n\n### 15. Final conclusion\n\n> “The impossibility arguments … together with the explicit constructions … prove that a configuration exists **iff** \\(k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\).”\n\n*Incorrect justification.*  \nBecause the impossibility arguments (steps 6–13) contain critical errors, the conclusion that **no other** values of \\(k\\) work is not rigorously established.  The answer happens to be correct, but the proof is not.\n\n--- \n\n**Overall assessment:** The solution reaches the correct set of admissible \\(k\\) values, but the logical chain contains several **Critical Errors** (false statements about \\(|p+q|\\), incomplete classification of slopes, incorrect parity claims) and **Justification Gaps** (the reduction to vertical non‑sunny lines).  Consequently the solution is not a valid proof."
